const CHAPTERS_B = [
{
  "id":"r4","unit":"ur2","number":4,
  "title":"Option One: Mark-Up-To-Market",
  "subtitle":"Entitlement vs discretionary MUTM, the 150% FMR cap, ownership tests, and how a MUTM request is processed.",
  "sections":[
  {"heading":"Why MUTM Exists — and the Two Doors Into It","body":"Mark-Up-To-Market began in June 1999 as an emergency initiative (paragraph 3-1). The problem it solved was specific: in strong rental markets, owners of Section 8 projects with BELOW-market rents had every incentive to leave the program, because the unassisted market would pay them more than HUD did. MUTM lets those owners bring Section 8 rents up to comparable market rents in exchange for staying in.\n\nThe universe of eligible projects is limited, and MAHRA splits it in two.\n\nOPTION ONE-A — ENTITLEMENT MUTM. Section 524(a)(4)(A) of MAHRA made MUTM permanent and REQUIRES HUD to mark up the rents of projects meeting the criteria in Section 3-3. If the project qualifies, the owner is entitled to the mark-up. HUD has no discretion to refuse.\n\nOPTION ONE-B — DISCRETIONARY MUTM. Section 524(a)(4)(C) gives HUD DISCRETIONARY authority to mark up rents for projects that do not qualify under One-A but meet the criteria in Section 3-6. The word that matters is \"discretionary\": HUD's stated practice is to approve projects meeting just one of the three Section 3-6 criteria, but if Section 8 appropriations run short, HUD may decide based on how many criteria a project meets.\n\nTHE GENERAL ELIGIBILITY GATE (3-2). Before either door opens, every MUTM project — One-A or One-B — must clear three general requirements:\n\nA. RENTS. The AGGREGATE current rents under the expiring (or terminating) contract must be BELOW comparable market rents. Not at; below. A project whose rents equal market has nothing to mark up.\n\nB. CONDITION AND MANAGEMENT. The project must have a Management and Occupancy Review (MOR) rating of Satisfactory or above, AND a REAC physical inspection score of 60 or higher with NO uncorrected Exigent Health and Safety (EH&S) violations.\n\nC. FINANCIAL. Any FASS (Financial Assessment Subsystem) findings must be closed, or under a HUD-approved corrective action plan.\n\nHOW TO READ \"AGGREGATE.\" The test is on the project's TOTAL current rent potential under the contract versus total comparable market rent potential — not unit type by unit type. A project whose two-bedroom rents happen to exceed market for that unit type can still be eligible if the aggregate is below market.\n\nWorked example — the gate. Linden Court: aggregate Section 8 rent potential $61,200 per month; RCS comparable market rent potential $68,900. MOR rating Satisfactory. REAC score 74, all EH&S items certified corrected. FASS findings from last year are under an approved corrective action plan. Linden Court clears 3-2. Whether it goes through door One-A or One-B depends on the next two sections.\n\nNow change one fact: the REAC score is 58. Linden Court fails 3-2.B and is not eligible for EITHER MUTM door, no matter how far below market its rents are. That project's realistic options are Option Two (Chapter 5 of this course) and fixing the property.\n\nCONSULTANT'S NOTE. The 3-2 gate is the cheapest test to run and the most expensive to miss. Pull the latest MOR letter, the REAC report and the FASS status BEFORE the owner commissions an RCS for a MUTM request. A rent comparability study takes weeks and real money; an owner who commissions one to pursue an option the project could never qualify for has spent both for nothing."},
  {"heading":"Option One-A: The Entitlement Tests","body":"A project that clears the general gate in 3-2 is an ENTITLEMENT MUTM project (Option One-A) only if it meets every test in Section 3-3.\n\nA. THE 100% FMR TEST. The RCS must show that the comparable market rents are at or above 100 PERCENT of the Fair Market Rent (FMR) potential. Use the FMRs for the fiscal year in which the project enters MUTM. HUD publishes Excel worksheets (\"Forms Associated with Section 8 Renewals\") that perform the comparison; the AE/CA uses iREMS to establish initial eligibility.\n\nRead the test carefully: it compares the MARKET (from the RCS) to the FMR. It is not a test of the project's current rents. The point is to confine entitlement status to strong markets — where comparable market rents meet or exceed the FMR.\n\nWorked example. Birchwood's RCS shows comparable market rent potential of $82,400 per month. The FMR potential for the same unit mix, using the FMRs for the year the project enters MUTM, is $79,100. $82,400 ≥ $79,100, so the 100% FMR test is met. If the market potential had been $77,800, Birchwood would fail 3-3.A and could only look to One-B.\n\nB. NO NON-ELIMINABLE USE RESTRICTION. The project must NOT be subject to a use restriction requiring low- or moderate-income occupancy that the owner cannot eliminate unilaterally. The Guide's examples: Flexible Subsidy, Low Income Housing Tax Credits (LIHTC), and Recap's Green Retrofit Program. The AE/CA determines whether a given restriction is the disqualifying type. Chapter 14 applies the same rule to RHS Section 515/8 projects: a use agreement the owner cannot eliminate unilaterally blocks One-A — but the owner may still be able to renew under One-B.\n\nC. NOT OLD MOD REHAB. The project must not be a Moderate Rehabilitation project under Section 8(e)(2) of the U.S. Housing Act as in effect BEFORE October 1, 1991.\n\nD. NO PRIOR VOUCHER EVENT. The project must not be one where the PHA provided voucher assistance to tenants after the owner gave notice of termination. An owner who started down the opt-out path far enough that tenants were vouchered out cannot come back for entitlement MUTM.\n\nE. OWNERSHIP. The owner must be one of:\n1. a profit-motivated entity, including a limited distribution entity;\n2. a housing authority that is a \"public body corporate and politic\" under its state enabling law;\n3. a limited partnership with one or more nonprofit general partners, or a sole general partner wholly owned and controlled by one or more nonprofits;\n4. a limited liability company with one or more nonprofit managers or managing members, or a sole manager wholly owned or controlled by one or more nonprofits.\nNonprofit-controlled for-profit entities (types 3 and 4) can renew under Option One OR Option Two. A purely nonprofit owner is not on the One-A list — it reaches MUTM only through One-B.\n\nE.2 — PROPOSED SALE. If the project is being sold, the PURCHASER must meet the ownership definition. The seller's status does not carry over.\n\nSource note: the Note under 3-3.E cites the nonprofit-controlled entity types as \"Section 3-2.D.1.c and d.\" Section 3-2 has no subsection D; the entity types are listed in 3-3.E.1.c and d.\n\nQUICK SCREEN — is this One-A?\n• Clears 3-2 (below-market aggregate, MOR Satisfactory+, REAC 60+ with no uncorrected EH&S, FASS clean or on a CAP)? \n• RCS market potential ≥ 100% of FMR potential?\n• No non-eliminable low/mod use restriction (no LIHTC, Flexible Subsidy, Green Retrofit)?\n• Not pre-October 1, 1991 8(e)(2) Mod Rehab?\n• No vouchers issued after a termination notice?\n• Owner (or purchaser) is for-profit, a public-body housing authority, or a nonprofit-controlled LP/LLC?\nSix yeses: entitlement. Any no: look at One-B."},
  {"heading":"The 150% FMR Cap and How to Exceed It","body":"Entitlement MUTM is generous, but it is capped (paragraph 3-4).\n\nTHE CAP. For Option One-A, Section 8 renewal rents are set at the LESSER of:\n• comparable market rents, or\n• 150 percent of FMR.\n\nThe comparison runs at the potential level, using the Guide's terms:\n• If the Final Comparable Gross Rent Potential is GREATER than 150% of the FMR potential → New Section 8 Gross Rents are set at 150% of FMR.\n• If the Final Comparable Gross Rent Potential is LESS than or equal to 150% of the FMR potential → New Section 8 Gross Rents are set at the Final Comparable Gross Rents.\n\nNote the word GROSS: the comparison includes utilities. Rents are compared and set as gross rents, then contract rents follow from the applicable utility allowances.\n\nWorked example 1 — under the cap. Maplewood's FMR gross potential is $70,000 per month; 150% is $105,000. The Final Comparable Gross Rent Potential is $93,500. $93,500 ≤ $105,000, so new Section 8 gross rents are set at the comparable gross rents: $93,500 potential.\n\nWorked example 2 — over the cap. Harbor View's FMR gross potential is $64,000; 150% is $96,000. The Final Comparable Gross Rent Potential is $118,400. Because $118,400 > $96,000, new Section 8 gross rents are capped at 150% of FMR: $96,000 potential. The owner leaves $22,400 a month of market rent on the table — unless the project can exceed the cap.\n\nTHE SCOPE OF A MARK-UP (3-4.B). MUTM affects only the Section 8 units in the contract (or stage) being renewed. It does not change rents on non-Section 8 units, and it does not change Section 8 units in a contract or stage that is not currently expiring.\n\nEXCEEDING THE CAP (3-5). Two facts govern:\n1. The 150% cap applies ONLY to Option One-A, and only where the \"lesser of\" works out to 150% of FMR.\n2. If a One-A project ALSO meets one of the three Option One-B criteria in Section 3-6.B — vulnerable populations, low vacancy, or community support — its renewal rents are set at comparable market rents, not at the cap.\n\nApply it to Harbor View. If at least 50 percent of its assisted units are rented to elderly households, it meets the vulnerable-populations criterion, and its rents are set at comparable market: $118,400 potential, not $96,000. The difference — $22,400 a month, $268,800 a year — turns on one occupancy statistic.\n\nTHE PRACTICAL LESSON. When a One-A project's RCS comes back above 150% of FMR, the next question is never \"how do we live with the cap?\" It is \"which One-B criterion can we document?\" Pull the rent roll (vulnerable populations), the most recent vacancy data for the market (low vacancy), and the project's funding history for the last five years (community support) before the owner signs anything.\n\nCOMMON ERROR. Applying the 150% cap to a One-B project. The cap is a One-A rule. A One-B project is marked up to comparable market rents (Section 3-6) — the cap question does not arise."},
  {"heading":"Option One-B: Discretionary MUTM and the Three Criteria","body":"Option One-B (paragraph 3-6) is for a project that is NOT eligible for One-A but meets the general requirements in 3-2 and the following criteria.\n\nHUD'S PRACTICE. HUD will generally mark up projects that meet ONLY ONE of the three criteria below. However, if there is a shortage of Section 8 appropriations, HUD may use its discretion to approve projects based on the NUMBER of criteria met. A project meeting all three is in a stronger position than one meeting one.\n\nNONPROFIT OWNERS. A nonprofit-owned project that meets one of the three criteria can qualify for One-B. This is the only path to MUTM for a purely nonprofit owner — and Chapter 15 of the Guide builds on it: a nonprofit owner or purchaser meeting one of the Section 3-6 criteria can use the preservation provisions when renewing under Option One.\n\nTHE THREE CRITERIA (3-6.B).\n\n1. VULNERABLE POPULATIONS. At least 50 PERCENT of the assisted units are rented to elderly families, disabled families, or large families (five or more persons). The 50 percent may be met by any one category or by all categories combined.\n\n2. LOW VACANCY. The project is in a low-vacancy area — or a rural area with no comparable rental housing — with a lack of affordable housing, where Housing Choice Vouchers are difficult to use. A rental vacancy rate of 3 PERCENT OR LESS, using the most recent data available, defines low vacancy. The AE/CA confirms with HUD's Economic and Market Analysis Section (EMAS).\n\n3. COMMUNITY SUPPORT. The project has had a contribution of State or local funds — tax credits, tax abatements, capital improvement funds and the like — provided within the LAST FIVE YEARS.\n\nWorked examples — counting the vulnerable-populations criterion:\n• 80 assisted units: 22 elderly, 9 disabled, 11 large families = 42 units = 52.5% → MET.\n• 120 assisted units: 31 elderly, 14 disabled, 12 large families = 57 units = 47.5% → NOT MET. (Check whether a household is counted twice — an elderly household with a disabled member is one unit, not two.)\n• 60 assisted units: 30 elderly → exactly 50% → MET (\"at least 50 percent\").\n\nWorked example — community support timing. A city capital improvement grant was disbursed six years before the MUTM request. It does not count; the window is the last five years. A state tax abatement renewed 18 months ago does count.\n\nWATCH THE INTERACTION WITH ONE-A. Note the community-support examples include TAX CREDITS. But a LIHTC use restriction that the owner cannot eliminate unilaterally DISQUALIFIES a project from One-A (3-3.B). So a tax credit project typically cannot be an entitlement project — yet the tax credit allocation itself may satisfy the One-B community-support criterion. Advising a LIHTC owner, the conversation goes straight to One-B.\n\nTHE DIFFERENCE IN RENT-SETTING. One-B projects are marked up to comparable market rents. The 150% FMR cap is a One-A device; it does not apply. In a market where comparable rents exceed 150% of FMR, a One-B project can therefore end up with higher rents than an otherwise identical One-A project that meets no One-B criterion — which is exactly why 3-5 lets One-A projects that ALSO meet a One-B criterion escape the cap."},
  {"heading":"Requesting, Processing and Living Under a MUTM Contract","body":"WHEN AN OWNER CAN ASK (3-7). An owner may request MUTM at any time, but how depends on the existing contract:\n1. DURING A SECTION 524 MAHRA CONTRACT: if approved, the owner may terminate early if the new MUTM contract is 20 YEARS and the owner agrees to the Preservation Exhibit — renewing at the end of the 20 years for a term equal to the remaining term of the original terminated contract, in whole months.\n2. UNDER A NON-MAHRA CONTRACT: the owner must renew for 20 years, agree to the Preservation Exhibit, and sign Form HUD-93184, the Rider to Original Section 8 HAP Contract.\nSource note: 3-7.A.1 speaks of the remaining term \"in whole months\"; Section 2-4.D rounds the Preservation Exhibit term to whole YEARS (down under six months, up at six months or more). Where the two conflict, confirm with the AE which convention the exhibit will use.\n\nWHO CANNOT TERMINATE EARLY FOR MUTM (3-7.B–D):\n• Demonstration or Preservation contracts (Option Five);\n• Full Mark-to-Market contracts (HUD-9642);\n• Rental Assistance Demonstration (RAD) contracts.\n\nPROCESSING (3-8). The steps in 2-17 apply, as modified:\nA. The AE/CA uses the owner's RCS to decide whether the project is One-A or One-B, reviewing it with a qualified appraiser under Sections 9-17 through 9-20. For a Chapter 15 transaction whose RCS shows both \"as-is\" and \"after-rehab\" rents, use the AS-IS rents to determine initial eligibility.\nB. If the project is NOT eligible for what the owner requested:\n  1. Not One-A but eligible for One-B → the AE/CA notifies the owner and asks it to modify the request.\n  2. Eligible for neither:\n    a. if the request came with an early-termination request, the owner may withdraw entirely and the existing contract stays in place;\n    b. otherwise the owner must choose another option for which the project is eligible — including Option Six — and if it chooses another option, the owner's RCS (as reviewed under Chapter 9) sets the market rents.\n  3. If the existing contract has expired, the AE/CA offers a short-term renewal at CURRENT rents while the owner decides.\nC. An RCS is required with every MUTM request.\n\nLIVING UNDER THE CONTRACT (3-9).\nA. NO budget-based rent increases under MUTM. In years two through five, rents are adjusted by OCAF (2-17.B or C).\nB. For terms longer than five years, follow the fifth-year procedure in 2-17.C.4: a new RCS at the end of each five-year cycle, with rents reduced to market if above and increased to market if below.\n\nRecall from Chapter 2 of this course: MUTM requires a minimum five-year term; Exhibit B (Distributions Limitation) is attached to all Option One contracts; and new regulation owners renewing under Option One are eligible for increased distributions even if the term is less than 20 years.\n\nWorked example — a mid-contract request. Sycamore Place is in year 3 of a 10-year Option Two contract. Market rents have climbed and the project now clears 3-2 and 3-3. The owner terminates by mutual agreement and signs a 20-year MUTM contract with a Preservation Exhibit covering the remaining 7 years of the terminated contract. Year 2–5 adjustments are OCAF only; at the end of year 5, a new RCS resets rents to market.\n\nWorked example — wrong door. A nonprofit owner (not nonprofit-controlled LP/LLC) requests One-A. The AE/CA finds the owner type ineligible for One-A, but 61% of assisted units house elderly or disabled households. The AE/CA asks the owner to modify the request to One-B rather than denying it."},
  ]
},
{
  "id":"r5","unit":"ur2","number":5,
  "title":"Option Two & Option Four: At-Market and Exception Renewals",
  "subtitle":"Who renews under Options Two and Four, the 'lesser of' test, Option Four's RCS rules, and the Section 202 refinancing traps.",
  "sections":[
  {"heading":"Option Two: The Default Renewal for At- or Below-Market Projects","body":"Option Two (Chapter 4 of the Guide) is the workhorse. It covers projects whose rents are at or below market but which are NOT renewing under MUTM — whether because they are not eligible, or because the owner prefers the stability of an OCAF-driven contract.\n\nELIGIBILITY (4-1.A). A project may renew under Option Two if its RCS shows EITHER:\n1. aggregate current rents at or BELOW comparable market rents; OR\n2. current rents EXCEED comparable market rents, BUT the project is EXEMPT from Mark-to-Market restructuring, and the owner is willing to reduce rents to market before renewal.\nNote in 4-1: projects currently renewed under Option Two or Option Four can reduce rents to market to renew under Option Two if they meet the early-termination rules in 2-4.B.\n\nThat second path is the counterpart of Option Two(b) from Chapter 1 of this course. It exists for owners of exempt, above-market projects who would rather take market rents under Option Two — for example, to qualify for a 20-year contract and the increased distributions that come with it — than stay on Option Four's \"lesser of\" track.\n\nOWNERS (4-1.B). Both nonprofit and for-profit owners may renew under Option Two. In a transfer, the PURCHASER must meet the definition of owner.\n\nRENT-SETTING AT RENEWAL (4-2).\nA. Follow the processing steps in 2-17.\nB. Rents are adjusted either by OCAF or, at HUD's DISCRETION, on a budget basis — with budget-based rents limited by comparable market rents.\n\nMULTI-YEAR CONTRACTS (4-3).\nA. In years two through five: OCAF, or at HUD's discretion budget-based (2-17.B or C).\nB. Terms longer than five years: the fifth-year procedure in 2-17.C.4 — new RCS, rents reduced to market if above, increased to market if below.\n\nPROCESSING (4-4). Renewals follow 2-17.A; adjustments follow 2-17.B (Auto OCAF) or C (non-Auto OCAF).\n\nWHAT HUD'S \"DISCRETION\" MEANS IN PRACTICE. An Option Two owner cannot demand a budget-based increase the way a One-A owner can demand a mark-up. The AE/CA reviews the budget under Handbook 4350.1 Chapter 7 as modified by 2-15 — the vacancy factors, the 1.2 maximum debt service coverage, the LIHTC limits — and the OCAF-adjusted RCS is the ceiling (2-17.C.3).\n\nWorked example. Cedar Hollow's RCS shows comparable market potential of $54,000 per month against current rents of $47,500. MUTM is off the table — the most recent REAC score of 58 fails the 3-2 gate. It renews under Option Two for 5 years with an OCAF adjustment. In year 3, a roof replacement drives a budget-based request producing rents of $52,800 — under the OCAF-adjusted market cap — and HUD, exercising its discretion, approves it.\n\nWorked example — the market ceiling. Same project, but the budget-based request produces $57,100 against an OCAF-adjusted market potential of $55,600. The AE/CA will not approve it; the owner receives OCAF or resubmits a budget limited to $55,600.\n\nWHERE OPTION TWO CONNECTS ELSEWHERE.\n• Increased distributions (2-12.D): for new regulation owners, available under Option Two only with a 20-YEAR contract.\n• Chapter 15 preservation transactions: nonprofit owners/purchasers use Option Two for Capital Repairs and Transfers (Chapter 16 of this course).\n• Section 9-4 alternatives: Method One (Small Area FMRs) is available ONLY for Option Two (Chapter 11 of this course)."},
  {"heading":"Option Four: Which Projects Are Exception Projects","body":"Option Four (Chapter 6 of the Guide) is for projects that cannot be referred to Recap. At expiration or termination, a project may renew under Option Four at \"exception rents\" if it is EITHER exempt from debt restructuring under Section 514(h) of MAHRA, OR not an eligible multifamily housing project under Section 512(2).\n\nA. EXEMPT UNDER 514(h) (6-1.A).\n1. STATE OR LOCAL GOVERNMENT FINANCING. The primary financing or mortgage insurance was provided by a unit of State or local government (or an agency or instrumentality of one), the project is ALSO insured under the National Housing Act, AND implementing a Mark-to-Market restructuring plan would conflict with applicable law or agreements governing that financing.\n   Note: State or locally financed projects that are NOT insured under the National Housing Act are not subject to Recap review at all.\n   Recap decides whether a conflict exists. The contract and documentation are referred to Recap, and the owner (or lender) submits an opinion of counsel acceptable to HUD with the financing documents or the State or local legal authority. Recap generally notifies the AE/CA within FIVE BUSINESS DAYS.\n   a. Not (or no longer) exempt → the AE/CA notifies the owner, who may proceed under 6-1.B.2 or select another option. A CA in this position returns the contract to the AE, recommending the AE forward it to Recap.\n   b. Conflict confirmed → Recap returns the contract to the AE/CA for Option Four renewal.\n2. SECTION 202/8 AND 515/8. Projects currently financed under Section 202 of the Housing Act of 1959 or Section 515 of the Housing Act of 1949. They can become eligible for restructuring if refinanced with FHA insurance. Note: Section 202 and 811 Capital Advance projects are not eligible here — they have no Section 8 contracts.\n3. Section 202 projects refinanced under Section 811 of the American Homeownership and Economic Opportunity Act of 2000 (see the next section).\n4. Single Room Occupancy (SRO) Mod Rehab projects under Section 441 of the McKinney Homeless Assistance Act.\n\nB. NOT AN ELIGIBLE PROJECT UNDER 512(2) (6-1.B).\n1. Projects not subject to an FHA-insured or HUD-held mortgage. Note: Risk-sharing projects under Section 542(b) and (c) of the Housing and Community Development Act of 1992 are eligible for Option Four (Chapter 5 of the Guide explains why: they do not meet the 512(2) definition).\n2. FHA-insured or HUD-held projects with rents AT OR BELOW comparable market rents. Note: the owner must obtain an RCS to establish eligibility under 6-1.B.2.\n\nTHE 6-1.B.2 TRAP. Everything else in Option Four is \"generally exempt from RCS.\" But a 6-1.B.2 project's ONLY claim to exception status is that its rents are at or below market — so it must prove that with an RCS. Chapter 2 of this course flagged the same carve-out: no RCS is required at initial or subsequent Option Four renewal \"unless the project is renewing under the criteria in Section 6-1.B.2.\"\n\nWorked examples — classify each:\n• An uninsured project financed by a conventional bank loan → 6-1.B.1: not eligible under 512(2) → Option Four, no RCS needed to establish eligibility.\n• An FHA-insured project with an RCS showing rents 4% below market → 6-1.B.2 → Option Four available, RCS required. (It is also eligible for Option Two.)\n• An FHA-insured project with rents 9% ABOVE market, state HFA-financed, where the state statute would conflict with an M2M plan → 6-1.A.1 if Recap confirms the conflict → Option Four.\n• The same project, but Recap finds no conflict → not exempt; with above-market rents and FHA insurance, it is an Option Three (Recap) project.\n• A Section 542(c) risk-sharing project → Option Four."},
  {"heading":"Option Four Rent-Setting: The 'Lesser Of' Test","body":"THE RULE AT RENEWAL (6-2.A). At both initial AND subsequent renewal, Option Four renewal rents are the LESSER of:\n1. current rents adjusted by OCAF; or\n2. a budget-based rent level.\nThe source is Section 524(b)(1) of MAHRA and 24 CFR 402.5(b). The Guide adds a blunt note: project rents MAY BE REDUCED. If the owner's budget supports rents below the OCAF-adjusted current rents, the budget wins — even if that is a cut.\n\nReminder (2-15.B): in a refinancing, \"current debt service\" is the debt service that takes effect when the new loan closes.\n\nWorked example 1 — OCAF is lower. Current rents $780 for a one-bedroom. OCAF 3.4% → $806.52. The owner's budget supports $831. Renewal rent = lesser = $806.52.\n\nWorked example 2 — budget is lower. Current rents $780. OCAF 3.4% → $806.52. A refinancing has cut debt service so much that the budget supports only $742. Renewal rent = lesser = $742 — a reduction of $38 per unit per month. An owner who did not run the budget before refinancing is surprised; a consultant who did is not.\n\nTHE RULE DURING A MULTI-YEAR CONTRACT (6-3). Different rule. At the annual rent adjustment during a multiyear Option Four contract, there is NO \"lesser of\" test (6-3.B). Rents are adjusted by OCAF, or by a budget-based adjustment:\nA. A budget-based adjustment sets a rent level to meet operating expenses under Handbook 4350.1 Chapter 7, with HUD-9635 submitted (first budget), using CURRENT debt service notwithstanding the handbook. And:\n  a. An RCS must be submitted with the request, or have been submitted within the preceding five years — not required for a \"0\" budget.\n  b. Previously submitted RCSs are valid for five years and adjusted annually by OCAF.\n  c. If the RCS shows current rents ABOVE market, the budget request is DENIED (except a $0 request) and the owner gets OCAF only.\n  d. If the proposed budget rents are at or below the RCS market rents, HUD may, at its discretion, approve a budget-based increase not to exceed market.\n  Note: for Section 515 projects, the AE/CA accepts an RHS-approved budget without review — but an RCS is required if the resulting rents exceed what OCAF would have produced (Chapter 15 of this course covers RHS).\n  Note: a Section 202 project with a written and signed \"Debt Service Savings Agreement\" may include the dollar amount specified in that agreement in its budget.\nC. Processing follows 2-17.B.\n\nWHY THE TWO RULES DIFFER. Section 524(b)(1) governs RENEWAL rents for exception projects, and it imposes \"lesser of.\" Once the multiyear contract is running, the annual adjustment is a contract adjustment, and the Guide applies the ordinary OCAF-or-budget mechanism — with the RCS as the ceiling.\n\nWorked example 3 — annual adjustment, above market. Year 3 of a 10-year Option Four contract. The owner's 2023 RCS, OCAF-adjusted, shows market of $865; current rents are $890. The owner submits a budget seeking $935. Denied under 6-3.A.c — current rents exceed market. The owner receives OCAF. (An Option Four project's rents can sit above market; Option Four exists precisely for exception projects that cannot be restructured. But they cannot climb further above market by budget.)\n\nWorked example 4 — annual adjustment, room under market. Same project, but the OCAF-adjusted market is $960 and current rents are $890. The budget supports $935. HUD may approve $935 at its discretion (≤ $960).\n\nCOMMON ERROR. Running \"lesser of\" at an annual adjustment. It applies at renewal — initial and subsequent — not at anniversaries within a multiyear contract."},
  {"heading":"Section 202 Refinancings: Who Keeps Option Four","body":"Section 202 elderly projects with Section 8 contracts are a large share of the Option Four universe, and their refinancings determine whether they stay there (6-4).\n\nA. COMPLIANT REFINANCING. A Section 202 project refinanced in compliance with Section 811 of the American Homeownership and Economic Opportunity Act of 2000 REMAINS EXEMPT from Mark-to-Market — even if it now has above-market rents and an FHA-insured mortgage.\n\nB. NON-COMPLIANT REFINANCING. A project that does not comply with Section 811 remains exempt UNLESS its Section 202 Direct Loan was refinanced with an FHA-insured mortgage when the HAP contract comes up for renewal.\n\nC. THE \"RE-REFINANCE.\" An owner that prepaid the original 202 loan and is now refinancing AGAIN:\n• with a CONVENTIONAL (non-FHA) loan → retains Option Four eligibility at the next contract expiration;\n• with an FHA-INSURED loan → is no longer exempt from Mark-to-Market and LOSES Option Four eligibility at the next expiration.\nIn both cases the project must continue to operate under its recorded Section 202 Use Agreement.\n\nD. Notice H 2013-17 (or successors) governs the details.\n\nThe Recap chapter adds the mirror-image rule (5-1.H): a Section 202 project refinanced a second time is ELIGIBLE for Recap only if (1) it was refinanced at least two times and the second refinance used a National Housing Act-insured loan, or (2) it was refinanced with an FHA-insured loan at the time of the 202 refinancing but not under the terms of Notice 13-17. All other 202 refinances are ineligible for Recap.\n\nAnd the prepayment rule from Chapter 5 of the Guide (5-4.E): an owner whose project was at Recap and who PREPAYS the mortgage after submitting HUD-9624 is NOT eligible for Option Four, now or in the future — except under the Section 202 refinancing provisions of 6-4.\n\nWorked examples:\n• Oakridge Senior (202/8) refinanced in 2012 under Section 811 with an FHA-insured loan; rents are now 7% above market. → Remains exempt (6-4.A). Option Four.\n• Elm Senior prepaid its 202 loan in 2010 and is now refinancing with a Fannie Mae conventional loan. → Retains Option Four at next expiration (6-4.C). Must keep operating under its 202 Use Agreement.\n• Pine Senior prepaid its 202 loan in 2010 and is now refinancing with a HUD 223(f) FHA-insured loan; rents are above market. → Loses Option Four eligibility at next expiration; with FHA insurance and above-market rents it becomes a Recap candidate.\n\nTHE CONSULTANT'S QUESTION. Before an elderly-housing client signs a refinancing term sheet, ask: is the new loan FHA-insured, and was the original 202 loan already prepaid? Those two answers decide whether the property stays on Option Four's exception-rent track or is exposed to Mark-to-Market. The financing team will be focused on rate and proceeds; somebody has to be focused on the HAP contract."},
  {"heading":"Choosing Between Options Two and Four — and Combining with Recap Rules","body":"Some projects are eligible for both Option Two and Option Four — most commonly an FHA-insured project with rents at or below market (6-1.B.2), or an uninsured project with below-market rents (6-1.B.1). The owner chooses. The trade-offs are real.\n\nTHE COMPARISON\n• RCS. Option Two needs an RCS at renewal and every five years. Option Four generally does not — except for 6-1.B.2 eligibility and annual budget requests.\n• RENEWAL RENT. Option Two: current rents adjusted by OCAF, or (at HUD's discretion) budget-based up to market. Option Four: the LESSER of OCAF-adjusted current rents or a budget — and rents can go down.\n• FIFTH-YEAR RESET. Option Two contracts over five years reset to market at each five-year RCS — up if below, down if above. Option Four has no five-year market reset.\n• ABOVE-MARKET RENTS. An Option Four project may carry rents above market (it is an exception project). Under Option Two, an above-market exempt project must cut to market first (4-1.A.2).\n• DISTRIBUTIONS. Increased distributions (2-12.D) are available under Option Two with a 20-year contract (or Option One). They are not listed for Option Four.\n• EARLY TERMINATION. An Option Four owner may terminate early only to renew again under Option Four for 20 years, if still eligible (2-4.B.4) — or reduce rents to market and move to Option Two (4-1 note).\n\nWorked decision 1. An uninsured family project with rents 15% below market and a for-profit owner seeking long-term cash flow: Option Two with a 20-year term (increased distributions, a market reset at year five) — or, if eligible, MUTM. Option Four's \"lesser of\" offers nothing here.\n\nWorked decision 2. An uninsured project whose rents are 12% ABOVE market, financed conventionally: Option Four is the natural home — rents stay above market, subject to \"lesser of\" at renewal. Moving to Option Two would require cutting rents to market first.\n\nWorked decision 3. A state-financed, FHA-insured project whose rents are above market and whose conflict-of-law claim Recap REJECTED: neither Option Two nor Option Four — it is an Option Three (Recap) project under 5-1.A, and must be referred.\n\nEXCEPTION PROJECTS AT RECAP. The Recap chapter closes the loop (5-1.E): exception projects, including Section 542(c) Risk Sharing projects, are INELIGIBLE for referral and will not be retained by Recap. Once the condition that makes a project an exception no longer exists — the state financing is paid off and replaced with FHA insurance, say — the project becomes eligible.\n\nTHE RULE OF THUMB. Option Four protects a project that would otherwise be cut; Option Two rewards a project that is under market. Model both before the owner signs HUD-9624."},
  ]
},
{
  "id":"r6","unit":"ur2","number":6,
  "title":"Option Five: Demonstration & Preservation Projects",
  "subtitle":"Demo use agreements, Preservation Plans of Action, opt-out limits, conflicting documents, and preservation-specific rents.",
  "sections":[
  {"heading":"What Counts as a Demo or Preservation Project","body":"Option Five (Chapter 7 of the Guide) covers two families of projects that went through earlier preservation programs and carry long-term commitments as a result.\n\nDEMONSTRATION (DEMO) PROJECTS (7-1). The Portfolio Reengineering Demonstration Program was an interim measure, pending a permanent program, authorized in FY 1996, 1997 and 1998. Restructurings were completed through 1999 into 2000; the Guide counts 188 Demo projects remaining. The rules in force when a project entered the Demo continue to apply; projects that did not meet the time limits had to leave, get an extension, or restructure under the authority in force when the time limits were reached.\n\nThe defining document: a project is a Demo project only if it COMPLETED the Demo Program, evidenced by a RECORDED Demo Program Use Agreement. A project that entered the Demo but never executed and recorded a Use Agreement is NOT eligible for Option Five (7-1, 7-2.B) — it may renew under any OTHER option it qualifies for.\n\nThe two types of Demo Use Agreement (7-2.A):\n• a Mortgage Restructuring Demo Program Use Agreement, or\n• a Budget-Based Without Mortgage Restructuring Demo Program Use Agreement.\n\nPRESERVATION PROJECTS (7-1). Primarily Section 236 and Section 221(d)(3) BMIR projects with long-term Use Agreements under:\n• Title II — the Emergency Low Income Housing Preservation Act of 1987 (ELIHPA), or\n• Title VI — the Low-Income Housing Preservation and Resident Homeownership Act of 1990 (LIHPRHA).\nEach was approved under a Plan of Action (POA).\n\nWHY THESE PROJECTS ARE LOCKED IN. Both families received something valuable — debt restructuring, budget-based rents, incentives to stay affordable — in exchange for long-term commitments. Chapter 2 of this course stated the rule: owners of Preservation and Demo projects must renew under Option Five for the life of the use agreement (2-3.C.2.c).\n\nDEMO OWNERS (7-2.A).\n1. Must renew under Option Five.\n2. Are required to ACCEPT HUD's offers to renew throughout the life of the Demo Use Agreement. Consequence: a Demo owner CANNOT opt out during the Use Agreement.\n\nPRESERVATION OWNERS (7-5.A). Must renew according to the approved POA — which includes the HUD approval letter and the recorded Preservation Use Agreement. They cannot renew under any option other than Option Five UNLESS the project is being transferred or sold, in which case the owner may request Mark-to-Market (Option Three) under 5-1.D — Recap decides whether to accept.\n\nNEITHER FAMILY GOES TO RECAP AS A MATTER OF COURSE. Demo contracts are not eligible for Recap processing (7-4.A); Preservation contracts are ineligible for Recap processing (7-7.A), the 5-1.D sale exception aside.\n\nWorked example — the missing recording. Willow Terrace entered the Demo in 1998 and its file contains an executed but UNRECORDED Mortgage Restructuring Demo Use Agreement. It is not a Demo project for Option Five purposes. If its rents are at or below market it may consider Options One or Two; if above market with an FHA-insured mortgage, Option Three. Recording status is a title search question — order one."},
  {"heading":"Renewing Demo Contracts","body":"INITIAL RENEWALS (7-3.A). The Portfolio Reengineering Demonstration was replaced by Mark-to-Market. HUD no longer processes INITIAL renewals under the Demo. Option Five Demo renewals are therefore all subsequent renewals of projects that completed the program.\n\nSUBSEQUENT RENEWALS (7-3.B).\n1. Under Option Five, for a term of at least one year and at most the LESSER of 20 years or the remaining life of the Demo Use Agreement. Note: the owner may request an extension of the Demo Use Agreement to facilitate a preservation transaction (7-4 note: for a refinancing transaction).\n2. THE EXCEPTION: a project with a recorded Budget-Based Without Mortgage Restructuring Demo Use Agreement MAY renew under Option One or Option Two, for a term of at least the remaining term of the project's Use Agreement and at most 20 years.\n\nRead that exception carefully. It applies only to the budget-based-WITHOUT-restructuring variety. A project with a Mortgage Restructuring Demo Use Agreement has no such exit; it renews under Option Five.\n\nRENT ADJUSTMENTS (7-3.C).\n1. Terms over five years: at the end of each five-year period, an RCS; rents below market are adjusted UP to market, rents above market are REDUCED to market.\n2. Years two through five: OCAF or budget-based.\nThis matches the 2-17.C.4 fifth-year rule (Chapter 3 of this course), which names Option Five Demo contracts alongside Options One and Two. Recall also from Chapter 3: Option Five Demos with restructured loans are on Auto OCAF for the life of the Use Agreement.\n\nPROCESSING (7-4.B). Follow 2-17.\n\nWorked example 1 — term limits. A Mortgage Restructuring Demo Use Agreement has 11 years 4 months remaining. The owner wants 20 years. Maximum Option Five term: the lesser of 20 years or 11 years 4 months → 11 years 4 months (and the contract must still expire on the last day of a month). To go longer, the owner asks HUD to extend the Use Agreement as part of a preservation transaction.\n\nWorked example 2 — the exception. A Budget-Based Without Mortgage Restructuring Demo Use Agreement has 6 years remaining; market rents are well above current rents. The owner may renew under Option One (if it meets 3-2 and 3-3 or 3-6) or Option Two, for a term between 6 years (the remaining Use Agreement term) and 20 years.\n\nWorked example 3 — the five-year reset. A 15-year Option Five Demo contract; at year 5 the RCS shows market of $1,050 against current rents of $1,110 → rents reduced to $1,050. At year 10, market $1,240 against current OCAF-adjusted rents of $1,140 → rents increased to $1,240.\n\nWHY THE RESET MATTERS TO A DEMO OWNER. Many Demo projects had their rents set on a budget basis in the late 1990s. Over a long contract, the five-year resets are where an owner's rents catch up — or get cut. Build the RCS calendar into the asset plan."},
  {"heading":"Renewing Preservation Contracts Under the Plan of Action","body":"THE POA GOVERNS (7-5). Preservation projects renew according to their approved Plan of Action. Most POAs allow budget-based adjustments, OCAF, or the Annual Adjustment Factor (AAF). Chapter 3 of this course noted the corollary: the OCAF-adjusted RCS market cap on budgets does NOT apply to Option Five preservation projects (2-17.C.3), and Title II and Title VI preservation projects follow the rent adjustment mechanism in their POA and/or Use Agreement (2-13.B).\n\nCORRECTIONS (7-5.B). Some preservation contracts were renewed in the past on terms that did not match the POA. The fix:\n• The AE/CA calculates the rent AS IF the contract had been renewed consistently with the POA. That figure becomes the \"current rent\" basis for the renewal.\n• HUD will NOT reimburse income the owner lost from past renewals.\n\nWorked example — a correction. A LIHPRHA POA calls for annual budget-based adjustments. Three prior renewals were processed with OCAF only, leaving rents at $910. Recomputed under the POA's budget-based mechanism, rents would be $985. For the upcoming renewal, $985 is the current-rent basis. The owner does not receive the cumulative shortfall from the three prior years.\n\nTERMS (7-6.A). A multiyear Preservation renewal cannot exceed the LESSER of 20 years or the remaining term of the recorded Use Agreement. Note: the owner may request a Use Agreement extension for a refinancing transaction.\n\nOPTIONS (7-6.B). Option Five is the only option — unless the project is transferred or sold, in which case M2M (Option Three) may be requested under 5-1.D.\n\nRENTS (7-6.C). Adjusted according to the POA and recorded Use Agreement.\n\nPROCESSING (7-6.D, 7-7.B). Follow 2-17.\n\nELIHPA CONTRACTS THAT END TOGETHER (7-9.C). Where a project's mortgage and ELIHPA Use Agreement end on the first of a month, or at midnight on the last day of a month, the Section 8 contract and the Use Agreement are coterminous. At that point the owner may select any option for which it is eligible — EXCEPT Option Five. The Use Agreement that locked the owner into Option Five has ended.\n\nBUDGET-BASED INCREASES FOR PRESERVATION PROJECTS (7-9.B).\n1. Instructions are in Chapter 11 of HUD Handbook 4350.6.\n2. Under Section 11-7.C.3 of that handbook, when non-operating costs change — for example, a loan is repaid — the budget is adjusted; HUD recognizes new debt in a budget request.\n3. If a budget item is deemed reasonable, it is not rolled back in a subsequent year.\n\nSHALLOW RENT SUBSIDY (7-9.A). The shallow rent subsidy depended on 1995 Appropriations Act language that was never made permanent. It no longer applies.\n\nRECAP AND PRESERVATION (5-1.D). A Preservation project owner may request Mark-to-Market debt restructuring any time before the M2M Sunset, ONLY if the project is being transferred or sold, and Recap decides whether to accept. This is the one door out of Option Five during the Use Agreement — and it requires a sale."},
  {"heading":"Opt-Outs and Conflicting Documents in Preservation Projects","body":"OPT-OUTS (7-5.C). Plans of Action generally do NOT permit opt-outs. But POAs differ, and an owner who believes its POA allows an opt-out has a process to follow:\n\n1. THE OWNER submits a Plan to the AE/CA showing how it will honor its Use Agreement obligations to maintain affordable housing:\n   a. how it will maintain an appropriate income mix; and\n   b. recognizing that opting out does NOT release the owner from its Use Agreement obligations.\n2. THE LOCAL OFFICE:\n   a. The AE/CA reviews the POA — the HUD approval letter and the recorded Use Agreement — to determine whether the owner has the right to opt out. Each POA is structured differently; there is no shortcut.\n   b. If the POA does not allow it → the owner must renew.\n   c. If it does → the owner follows the standard one-year notice to tenants, and eligible families receive ENHANCED vouchers.\n3. If the AE/CA agrees the owner can opt out, it forwards a copy of the owner's plan to the Director of the Office of Asset Management and Portfolio Oversight (OAMPO) at Headquarters for approval or rejection.\n\nThe structure tells you something: even where the local office reads the POA to allow an opt-out, Headquarters makes the final call.\n\nCONFLICTING DOCUMENTS (7-5.D). A POA and a Use Agreement sometimes disagree.\n1. The USE AGREEMENT is recorded and binding on third parties, so where the two conflict, the Use Agreement PREVAILS.\n2. For items in the POA that the Use Agreement does not address, the POA's terms are binding.\n\nWorked example. Pinecrest's POA says the owner may adjust rents by AAF \"or other method approved by HUD.\" Its recorded Use Agreement limits rent increases to amounts approved under the budget-based process. On rent adjustments, the Use Agreement prevails: budget-based. The POA also provides for a resident services coordinator; the Use Agreement is silent on services. The POA's services commitment is binding.\n\nWorked example — an opt-out request. Fairmont, a LIHPRHA project, asks to opt out. Its recorded Use Agreement requires affordability for the remaining useful life of the project. The owner's plan proposes to keep 40% of units affordable after opting out. The AE/CA reads the POA and Use Agreement and finds no opt-out right → the owner must renew. Even if the POA had contained an opt-out right, the plan's 40% proposal would have to square with the Use Agreement's affordability requirement — and opting out would not release the owner from it.\n\nCONSULTANT'S CHECKLIST for any Option Five project:\n☐ Is the Use Agreement RECORDED? (Demo: no recording, no Option Five.)\n☐ Demo type: Mortgage Restructuring, or Budget-Based Without Mortgage Restructuring?\n☐ Remaining Use Agreement term (caps the renewal term)?\n☐ Rent mechanism in the POA and Use Agreement — and do they conflict?\n☐ Any past renewals inconsistent with the POA (7-5.B correction)?\n☐ Is a sale contemplated (the 5-1.D M2M door)?\n☐ ELIHPA mortgage and Use Agreement ending together (7-9.C exit)?"},
  {"heading":"Preservation Section 8 Rents (PSRs)","body":"Preservation projects carry a concept no other option uses: the Preservation Section 8 Rent, or PSR (7-8).\n\nORIGIN. PSRs first appeared in Housing Notice 94-42, Mid-Course Correction II (MCCII), for LIHPRHA and ELIHPA projects, and in revisions to that notice.\n\nDEFINITION (7-8.A). A PSR is the rent that UNSUBSIDIZED tenants would reasonably be expected to pay in preservation properties, for the subject unit, based on the actual project and its conditions. It is derived from a market comparability analysis ADJUSTED to the actual project's characteristics — including repairs to be completed.\n\nThe distinction from an ordinary RCS market rent is the adjustment to the actual project, including planned repairs. A PSR is a market-derived rent for THIS property as it is or will be repaired — not a generic comparable-unit rent.\n\nMAINTENANCE (7-8.B). PSRs are to be maintained and updated annually during the annual rent increase. Notices 94-42 and 95-56 failed to identify a methodology for doing so. The Guide's answer:\n• the AE adjusts PSRs by the annual OCAF; and\n• the owner MAY submit a new PSR analysis at any time during the life of the Use Agreement if the PSRs affect total tenant payment — supported by market comparables, adjusted, and reviewed by the Multifamily Regional Office's valuation staff.\n\nWHY AN OWNER WOULD BOTHER. The phrase \"if the PSRs affect total tenant payment\" is the clue. In many preservation projects the PSR feeds into what certain tenants pay — for example, as a cap or benchmark for unassisted or partially assisted households under the Use Agreement. If OCAF adjustments have let PSRs drift away from the actual market, a fresh PSR analysis corrects them.\n\nWorked example 1 — annual update. A PSR of $1,020 with an OCAF of 2.9% → $1,020 × 1.029 = $1,049.58.\n\nWorked example 2 — new analysis. After five years of OCAF updates a project's two-bedroom PSR is $1,140, but recently completed repairs and a strong local market support $1,260 from adjusted comparables. Because the PSR affects total tenant payment for the project's preservation-restricted households, the owner submits a new PSR analysis; the Regional Office valuation staff reviews it.\n\nPUTTING OPTION FIVE TOGETHER. Demo projects: renew under Option Five for the lesser of 20 years or the remaining Use Agreement; five-year RCS resets; Budget-Based Without Restructuring Demos may move to Options One or Two. Preservation projects: renew under the POA; rents per the POA and Use Agreement, with no OCAF-adjusted market cap on budgets; Use Agreement beats POA in a conflict; opt-outs are rare and need Headquarters approval; PSRs are updated by OCAF unless the owner brings a new analysis. And for both: no Recap processing, except a Preservation sale under 5-1.D."},
  ]
},
];

