const CHAPTERS_C = [
{
  "id":"r7","unit":"ur3","number":7,
  "title":"Option Three: Referral to Recap",
  "subtitle":"Who must go to Recap, once-eligible-always-eligible, Lite vs Full, interim contracts, and how restructured contracts are executed.",
  "sections":[
  {"heading":"Mandatory Referral, the Sunset, and 'Once Eligible, Always Eligible'","body":"Option Three (Chapter 5 of the Guide) is where above-market projects with federal mortgages go. The Office of Recapitalization (Recap) — formerly the Office of Affordable Housing Preservation (OAHP) — processes them under the Mark-to-Market (M2M) program, and Recap's own policies govern waivers of Chapter 5.\n\nTHE SUNSET (5-1.A). The Guide states that M2M statutory authority expires October 1, 2017 unless extended by statute. Before the Sunset, owners of projects with:\n• an expiring HAP contract,\n• rents ABOVE comparable market rents, and\n• an FHA-insured or Secretary-held mortgage,\nMUST be referred to Recap, unless the project is exempt under Section 514(h).\nSource note: the March 2023 Guide still recites the October 1, 2017 date, although Congress has extended M2M authority by later legislation. Confirm the current Sunset date with Recap before relying on any Sunset-dependent rule.\n\nThe referral is mandatory, not optional. Recall the note in 5-3: if HUD determines that rents exceed market and the project is otherwise eligible, the AE MUST forward it to Recap; a CA that makes the determination returns the contract to the AE with a recommendation to refer.\n\nONCE ELIGIBLE, ALWAYS ELIGIBLE (5-1.B). Under Section 512(2)(C) as amended, before the Sunset, an FHA-insured or Secretary-held project whose rents were above market at its INITIAL renewal under Section 524 (on or after October 1, 1998) is eligible for referral and may request debt restructuring — even though it was previously renewed under 524, and even if its rents are NOW at or below market. It must still have a current project-based HAP contract and an FHA-insured or HUD-held mortgage.\n\nPREVIOUSLY AT-MARKET, NOW ABOVE (5-1.C). A project previously renewed under 524 whose rents were not above market then, but are above market now, is eligible before the Sunset.\n\nPRESERVATION PROJECTS (5-1.D). May request M2M debt restructuring before the Sunset ONLY if being transferred or sold; Recap decides.\n\nEXCEPTION PROJECTS (5-1.E). Ineligible for referral and not retained by Recap — including Section 542(c) Risk Sharing projects, which renew under Option Four. Once the exempting condition no longer exists, the project becomes eligible.\n\nMOD REHAB (5-1.G). Mod Rehab projects are eligible before the Sunset if they meet 5-1.B; they enter on the Interim (Full) M2M Renewal Contract, HUD-9640. (Chapter 1 of this course covered the Section 441 McKinney carve-out.)\n\nSECTION 202 SECOND REFINANCINGS (5-1.H). Eligible only if (1) refinanced at least twice where the second refinance uses an NHA-insured loan, or (2) refinanced with an FHA-insured loan at the time of the 202 refinancing but not under Notice 13-17. All other 202 refinances are ineligible.\n\nWorked example — once eligible. Riverbend's rents were 14% above market at its initial 524 renewal in 1999; it renewed as a Lite and its rents were cut to market. Today its rents are 3% below market and it still has an FHA-insured mortgage and a project-based contract. Before the Sunset, Riverbend may still request Full M2M debt restructuring — eligibility attached in 1999 and stays."},
  {"heading":"Suspended or Debarred Owners and the Binding Commitment","body":"SUSPENDED OR DEBARRED OWNERS (5-1.F). An owner who is suspended or debarred is INELIGIBLE for Full M2M — but may remain eligible for a Lite, because a Lite involves no restructuring: the project is financially viable without restructuring once rents are reduced to market.\n• The project is referred to Recap for a rent determination.\n• Lite eligibility is case-by-case, after an underwriting review of the RCS results and an expense analysis.\n• If the project is ineligible for a Lite and the owner or project is rejected, Recap returns the HAP contract to the AE.\n• If the contract is renewed anyway, the AE/CA renews with the WATCH LIST CONTRACT (HUD-9643) at the comparable market rents Recap determined. In iREMS the loan is designated as having failed M2M restructuring and operating under a Watch List contract, and OAMPO's monitoring guidance applies.\n\nThe submission rules repeat the point (5-3.C). For a Full, the Secretary may reject the request of a suspended or debarred OWNER unless the restructuring includes a transfer to a HUD-approved purchaser; if only an AFFILIATE is suspended or debarred, the project may continue to be eligible at the Secretary's discretion.\n\nTHE BINDING COMMITMENT (5-2). Processing may continue after the Sunset if a binding commitment was made before it. Two documents qualify:\n• the Renewal Worksheet for Option Three (part of HUD-9624), when executed by the owner AND an authorized HUD representative; or\n• a fully executed Interim (Full) M2M Contract.\nThe commitment continues so long as the owner and project remain eligible, subject to MAHRA, the Regulations, and Recap's Operating Procedures Guide (OPG).\n\nWorked example — timing. An owner signs the Option Three Renewal Worksheet on its HUD-9624 package two weeks before the Sunset; the HUD representative signs it the following week, still before the Sunset. That is a binding commitment, and restructuring can close after the Sunset. If the HUD signature had come after the Sunset and no Interim (Full) contract had been executed, there would be no binding commitment.\n\nWHY CONSULTANTS CARE ABOUT THE SUNSET EVEN NOW. Several Option Three rules — Watch List re-entry, once-eligible-always-eligible, Preservation sales — are framed as \"prior to Sunset.\" If you are advising on a long-dated strategy, the Sunset date and whether your client has a binding commitment are threshold questions."},
  {"heading":"Lite vs Full: The Two Restructuring Paths","body":"An above-market project referred to Recap before the Sunset may request one of two paths (5-3.A).\n\nOPTION 3A — LITE. Renewal WITHOUT debt restructuring. Rents are reduced to comparable market rents. The contract is the Interim (Lite) M2M Renewal Contract, HUD-9641, for a term Recap determines, NOT TO EXCEED 12 MONTHS. RENT ADJUSTMENTS ARE PROHIBITED during the Interim (Lite).\n\nOPTION 3B — FULL. Debt restructuring AND renewal (term up to 20 years), with rents reduced to market. Entry is on the Interim (Full) contract; NO rent adjustments during the Interim (Full).\n\nThe Lite fits a project that can carry its debt at market rents. The Full fits a project that cannot: reducing rents to market would leave too little to pay the mortgage, so the debt is restructured.\n\nCURRENTLY AT-MARKET PROJECTS (5-3.B). Before the Sunset, any project with above-market rents at its initial renewal on or after October 1, 1998 may be eligible for a Full even if current rents are now at or below market:\n1. WATCH LIST owners may request re-entry; for restructuring after the Sunset they need a binding commitment before it. If approved, the Watch List contract is terminated and an Interim (Full) issued.\n2. Owners originally eligible whose rents were reduced WITHOUT restructuring — including previously approved Lites — may request entry before the Sunset, on an Interim (Full).\n\nWHAT THE OWNER SUBMITS (5-3.C).\n1. LITE: HUD-9624 with a certification that rents exceed comparable market (or exceeded it on or after October 1, 1998) and that neither the owner nor an affiliate is suspended or debarred (a suspended/debarred owner may still be eligible for a Lite — see OPG Chapter 6, \"Approvals, Ineligibility and Appeals\"). Also: (a) a physical inspection report, (b) the most recent audited financial statements (official electronic submissions to HUD), and (c) an RCS. The AE/CA executes the Interim (Lite) for the term Recap determines, ≤12 months.\n2. FULL: HUD-9624 with the same certification. A suspended/debarred owner may be rejected unless the restructuring transfers the project to a HUD-approved purchaser; a suspended/debarred affiliate is at the Secretary's discretion.\n\nTHE INTERIM (FULL) CONTRACT (5-3.D.1). The AE/CA executes it and forwards it to Recap. It expires on the EARLIEST of:\n1. 12 months;\n2. the last day of the month in which closing occurs under the Restructuring Commitment; or\n3. a Final Determination resulting in discontinuance of the restructuring, or owner default.\n\nOther entry contracts (5-3.D.2–3): a Watch List contract (HUD-9643) re-entering is terminated and replaced with an Interim (Full) for the period Recap sets; a completed Lite returning for restructuring gets an Interim (Full) for a specific period (12 months or less) set by Recap.\n\nWorked example — Lite or Full? Aspen Court's rents are 18% above market. At market rents, net operating income falls to $412,000 against annual debt service of $455,000. It cannot carry its debt at market — a Full. Birch Point's rents are 6% above market; at market, NOI is $610,000 against debt service of $380,000. It can carry the debt — a Lite."},
  {"heading":"Interim Extensions and Mid-Process Exits","body":"EXTENSIONS (5-3.E). Restructurings sometimes take longer than a year. Who caused the delay decides the rent:\n1. RECAP, PAE OR HUD DELAY. The AE, Recap, or the Participating Administrative Entity (PAE) may request an extension of the interim contract at ABOVE-MARKET rents for a project taking longer than one year — not to exceed the approved expiration date, using the appropriate interim contract.\n2. OWNER DELAY. NO extension at above-market rents. The AE/CA extends the Interim (Full) at MARKET rents, for a period Recap directs as sufficient to bring the project to closing.\n\nWorked example. Cypress Gardens' Interim (Full) expires at 12 months. The PAE has not finished underwriting because its environmental reviewer was reassigned — a PAE delay. The interim is extended at the current above-market rents. Had the delay come from the owner failing to deliver its third-party reports, the extension would be at market rents — a cut of whatever the above-market premium was, every month until closing.\n\nAlso recall from Chapter 2 of this course (2-8.A.2): a project referred to Recap that has not closed under a Restructuring Plan after a year may be extended at current rents by short-term renewal; beyond a year on those terms requires Recap approval under 24 CFR 401.600. And short-term renewals of 514(c) contracts for Recap-bound projects are at CURRENT rent — no OCAF proration (2-9.C.1).\n\nOWNER OPTS OUT WHILE AT RECAP (5-4.D). Once the Regional Center confirms the opt-out, Recap completes its rent determination, informs the AE, and ceases processing. If time is needed for vouchers or tenant notification, the AE/CA prepares a ONE-YEAR Basic Renewal Contract (HUD-9636) at the comparable market rents Recap determined. Chapter 9 of this course covers the extra 120-day tenant notice for an owner who opts out during restructuring.\n\nOWNER PREPAYS AFTER SUBMITTING HUD-9624 (5-4.E). Once the Regional Center confirms the prepayment, Recap completes the rent determination, informs the AE, and ceases processing. Two consequences:\n• the AE prepares a Basic Renewal Contract at the comparable market rents Recap determined; and\n• the project is NOT eligible for Option Four, now or in the future — except under the Section 202 refinancing rules in 6-4.\n\nThat second consequence is easy to miss. A project that leaves Recap by paying off its FHA loan no longer has an insured mortgage — which would ordinarily make it a 512(2) exception project. The Guide closes that door: an owner cannot use a prepayment to escape restructuring and land on Option Four's exception rents.\n\nINTERIM PROCESSING (5-4.F) for Lites, Fulls and Watch List contracts follows Multifamily policies for project-based Section 8 renewals. For PBCA contracts, the Financial Management Center (FMC) executes the ACC and fund reservation (5-4.G). Contract forms are listed in 2-6.A (5-4.H)."},
  {"heading":"After Recap: Executing the Lite and the Full","body":"After Recap processes a project (5-4.A):\n1. LITES are renewed under Section 524. In multiyear contracts, annual adjustments are by OCAF or, if HUD approves, budget-based.\n2. FULLS are renewed under Section 515 upon closing, generally for a term of up to 20 years. Three hard rules:\n   • NO budget-based adjustments during the Full M2M contract or its renewals;\n   • the contract is NEVER terminated prematurely for any other option — including MUTM;\n   • owners are required to combine contracts, including later-expiring ones, under the Full M2M contract.\n\nEXECUTING A LITE (5-4.B.1).\n• Recap notifies the PAE and owner of approval and emails OAMPO's Assisted Housing Division Director requesting funding, with an Exhibit A (unit number and type, rents, utility allowances, monthly totals).\n• After notice of funding (by email or Notice of Funding Authorization), Recap completes the Basic Renewal Contract (HUD-9636 or HUD-9637) and forwards it to the PAE for owner signature, with an electronic copy to the AE.\n• The PAE sends it to the owner, who returns it to the AE/CA for HUD execution.\n\nEXECUTING A FULL (5-4.B.2).\n• Recap emails the OAMPO AHD Director early notice that the restructuring plan is approved and the Restructuring Commitment issued, with Exhibit A.\n• When the PAE reports that the owner has signed the Restructuring Commitment, Recap makes the official funding request (amount, tentative closing date, updated Exhibit A).\n• After funding notification, Recap completes the Full M2M contract → PAE → owner → AE/CA for HUD execution BEFORE the M2M closing. Copies go to the AE, the OAMPO AHD Director, and the Headquarters Closing Coordinator.\n\nPOST-CLOSING (5-4.C). Recap's Regional Preservation Office provides a \"Closing Docket Transmission Memorandum\" to the Multifamily Regional Center and records the closing in the Recap Management Information System (OPG Chapter 7, Closing; Chapter 8, Post-Closing Document Distribution).\n\nCONTACTS (5-7). The OPG is online. M2M contacts are the Debt Restructuring Specialists (DRS) at Recap's Regional Preservation Offices in Washington, DC and Chicago.\n\nTHE LITE vs FULL AFTERLIFE — the key contrast for Chapter 8 of this course:\n• A Lite becomes an ordinary 524 contract. At its subsequent renewal (generally five years), the owner may choose ANY option for which the project is eligible (5-5.A).\n• A Full is locked: Option Three, Section 515 contract (HUD-9642), OCAF only, no RCS, no early exit, for the life of the M2M Use Agreement.\n\nWorked example. Two neighboring projects went through Recap in 2015. Elmhurst took a Lite; at its 2020 subsequent renewal its rents had fallen below market, and it renewed under MUTM. Fairview took a Full; even though its rents are now well below market, it cannot use MUTM or any other option while its MTM Use Agreement runs — it renews under Option Three with OCAF only."},
  ]
},
{
  "id":"r8","unit":"ur3","number":8,
  "title":"Life After Restructuring: Full M2M Renewals & the Watch List",
  "subtitle":"Subsequent Full M2M renewals, use agreement terms, early termination for refinancing, exception rents, and Watch List contracts.",
  "sections":[
  {"heading":"Subsequent Renewals of a Full M2M Contract","body":"A Full M2M project lives under two documents: the Full M2M Renewal Contract (HUD-9642), issued under Section 515 of MAHRA, and the M2M Use Agreement. Section 5-5 governs what happens when the first Full contract expires.\n\nTHE INITIAL FULL CONTRACT (5-5.B). Executed at closing, generally for 20 years (it may be anywhere from 1 to 20 years).\n\nTHE USE AGREEMENT TERM. Section 514(e)(6) of MAHRA requires the M2M Use Agreement to run AT LEAST 30 YEARS. Subsequent renewals are under Option Three B, up to the remaining term of the Use Agreement. The initial contract plus all renewals may NOT extend beyond the Use Agreement.\n\nTHE OWNER MUST ACCEPT. If the Use Agreement is still in effect and the Secretary offers a renewal for a term no longer than the remaining Use Agreement term, on the same terms and conditions, the owner is OBLIGATED to accept. There is no opt-out from a Full during its Use Agreement.\n\nTHE RENEWAL CONTRACT. Must use the same form, HUD-9642. Rents are set at the levels under the expiring contract; the Full M2M contract permits only annual OCAF adjustments.\n\nThe Guide's Example 1: a 20-year Full contract expires; HUD offers a 10-year Full renewal at existing rents plus OCAF. The owner must accept — the Use Agreement has not expired and the term does not exceed what remains. Only OCAF applies.\n\nDURING THE USE AGREEMENT — the hard rules:\n• NO RCS is allowed or required;\n• NO budget-based adjustments;\n• the contract is NEVER terminated prematurely for any other option, including MUTM;\n• ALL renewals use HUD-9642.\n\nCompare Chapter 2 of this course: an RCS is not required at any subsequent renewal of an MTM \"Full\" contract during the MTM use agreement (2-5.E note), and Option Three is the only subsequent option for a Full M2M contract (2-11).\n\nWorked example — the math of terms. Ironwood closed its Full M2M on July 1, 2008 with a 30-year Use Agreement (to June 30, 2038) and a 20-year contract (to June 30, 2028). In 2028 HUD offers a 10-year renewal at the then-current rents plus OCAF. Ironwood must accept: 20 + 10 = 30, exactly the Use Agreement. HUD could not offer — and Ironwood could not demand — a 12-year renewal; that would run past the Use Agreement.\n\nWHY OWNERS RESIST, AND WHY IT DOESN'T MATTER. By year 20, many Full M2M properties sit in markets where comparable rents have risen far above their OCAF-indexed contract rents. Owners ask for MUTM, or a fresh RCS. The Guide's answer is categorical: during the Use Agreement, no RCS, no budget, no other option. The trade-off was made at closing, when HUD restructured the debt."},
  {"heading":"Terminating Early to Refinance a Full M2M Project","body":"Owners refinancing a Full M2M project's first mortgage often need a longer remaining HAP term — lenders commonly condition the loan on it (5-5.C). The Guide permits a narrow path.\n\nTHE PRELIMINARIES.\n• Refinancing triggers the due-on-sale/refinance clause in all M2M mortgages (the M2M second and third mortgages). Waivers are governed by Notice H 2012-10 or its successor.\n• EXTENSIONS of a Full M2M contract are NOT permitted. But the contract may be TERMINATED AND RENEWED.\n\nTHE CONDITIONS. HUD grants an early termination and renewal only if:\n• the owner agrees to EXTEND the Use Agreement term, if necessary, so it equals the term of the renewed contract;\n• rents are adjusted by OCAF only; and\n• the renewal uses HUD-9642.\n\nThe Guide's Example 2: five years into a 10-year renewal, the lender requires at least 10 years remaining on the HAP contract. The owner requests a 5-year Use Agreement extension (to 35 years) and early termination. The third contract runs 10 years. 20 + 5 + 10 = 35 = the extended Use Agreement. OCAF only for all 35 years.\n\nTHE ANNIVERSARY RESET. The anniversary date is based on the START of the new renewal contract, so the OCAF date resets — and there may be MORE than 12 months between OCAFs. HUD agrees to the termination only if the owner accepts that effect.\n\nThe Guide's Example 3: anniversary January 1, OCAF received January 1; terminated and renewed July 1 of the same year with initial rents equal to the then-current rents. The next OCAF comes the following July 1 — 18 months after the last one.\n\nWorked example — build it yourself. Juniper Place closed its Full M2M in 2010: 20-year contract (2010–2030), 30-year Use Agreement (to 2040). In 2024, six years before the first contract ends, Juniper refinances and the lender requires 15 years of remaining HAP term. Remaining Use Agreement: 2024 to 2040 = 16 years. A 15-year contract (2024–2039) fits within it, so no extension is needed. The contract is terminated and renewed for 15 years on HUD-9642, OCAF only. If its OCAF anniversary had been March 1 and the new contract starts September 1, the next OCAF comes September 1 of the following year — 18 months after the last.\n\nNow change the lender: it requires 20 years. 2024 + 20 = 2044, four years past the Use Agreement. Juniper must agree to extend the Use Agreement by 4 years, to 2044 — and the property is OCAF-only to the end.\n\nTHE CONSULTANT'S CHECK. Before a Full M2M owner signs a refinancing term sheet: (1) how many years remain on the Use Agreement? (2) how many years of HAP term does the lender require? (3) will the gap force a Use Agreement extension? (4) when does the OCAF anniversary move, and can the budget live with a long gap? (5) has the due-on-refinance waiver under H 2012-10 been requested?"},
  {"heading":"Exception Rents and the End of the Use Agreement","body":"EXCEPTION RENTS (5-5.D). Some Full M2M projects were restructured with rents ABOVE market — \"exception rents\" under Section 514(g) of MAHRA — because market rents could not support the project. These projects get a stricter rule:\n• they are NEVER renewed beyond the original Use Agreement term;\n• the Use Agreement is NOT extended beyond 30 years from the M2M restructuring;\n• early termination and renewal are permitted ONLY if the combined terms do not exceed 30 years.\n\nThe Guide's Example 4: five years into a 10-year renewal — year 25 after restructuring — a project with exception rents requests a new 10-year contract. REJECTED: 25 + 10 = 35 years, beyond 30.\n\nWorked example. Laurel Commons has exception rents; it restructured 22 years ago. It asks to terminate and renew for 8 years to support a refinancing. 22 + 8 = 30 → permitted. A request for 9 years (31) would be rejected, and unlike an ordinary Full M2M project, Laurel cannot fix the problem by extending its Use Agreement.\n\nWhy the difference? A non-exception Full M2M project's rents are at market; extending its Use Agreement buys more affordability at market cost. An exception-rent project's rents are ABOVE market; extending it would extend an above-market subsidy. The statute limits that exposure to 30 years.\n\nAFTER THE USE AGREEMENT (5-5.E). Except for certain qualified nonprofit transactions running longer than 30 years, renewals after 30 years from the M2M restructuring (where the Use Agreement was not extended under 5-5.C) proceed under the statutes and regulations then in effect, under ANY option for which the project is eligible.\n\nSo the Full M2M lock has an end: when the Use Agreement expires, the project re-enters the general renewal framework. A former M2M property with below-market rents in year 31 may look at MUTM; one with above-market rents and FHA insurance at that point is analyzed under whatever Recap authority then exists.\n\nPUTTING THE FULL M2M RULES TOGETHER\n• Initial contract: up to 20 years, Section 515, HUD-9642.\n• Use Agreement: at least 30 years (514(e)(6)).\n• Renewals: Option Three B, HUD-9642, OCAF only, no RCS, no budget, no other option, owner must accept HUD's offer.\n• Refinancing: terminate and renew (no extensions), extend the Use Agreement if needed, OCAF only, anniversary resets.\n• Exception rents: never beyond 30 years total, no Use Agreement extension.\n• After the Use Agreement: any eligible option under then-current law."},
  {"heading":"The Watch List Contract","body":"The Watch List is where projects go when M2M restructuring fails (5-6). It is used when:\n1. Recap decides the project is ineligible under Section 516(a) of MAHRA;\n2. the owner refuses to switch from a Lite to a Full after Recap determines that renewal without restructuring is insufficient for debt service coverage and/or physical integrity;\n3. the owner refuses to execute the Restructuring Commitment or to close the Full; or\n4. Recap concludes that restructuring will not produce an economically and financially feasible project.\n(OPG Chapter 6.)\n\nTHE CONTRACT. The Watch List Renewal Contract, HUD-9643, is issued in ONE-YEAR TERMS ONLY. Multiyear terms are not permitted (HUD-9643 Section 4d(2)).\n\nPROCESSING (5-6.B).\n1. After the PAE finishes, Recap prepares the one-year HUD-9643 and copies the AE/CA. The contract REDUCES RENTS TO MARKET as determined by Recap. The owner submits MONTHLY accounting reports. In iREMS the loan is designated as having failed M2M restructuring, and a designated Account Resolution Specialist monitors it.\n2. The project stays on the Watch List for THREE YEARS — three one-year contracts — unless removed. NO rent adjustments during each contract; OCAF is permitted BETWEEN contracts (for example, OCAF for the second and third contracts' rents).\n3. CONTINUED M2M ELIGIBILITY: an owner that chooses a Full submits HUD-9624 to the AE/CA; Recap may reassess; if approved, an Interim (Full) is executed at current market rents. A binding commitment before the Sunset is required.\n4. REMOVAL — only if:\n   (a) the owner requests to return to Recap and Recap approves;\n   (b) after three years on the list, the Account Resolution Specialist determines there has been no physical, financial or managerial deterioration;\n   (c) the owner prepaid the mortgage; or\n   (d) the owner opted out.\n   A Regional Center may request removal by written justification to Recap Headquarters.\n\nMONITORING (5-6.C–E). Watch List projects are at greater risk and are monitored under Handbook 4350.1. The PAE returns the files to the AE, who enters in iREMS's problem statement screen the monitoring category and reason. If the project deteriorates, the AE/CA acts immediately under 4350.1 — and Chapter 13 of the Guide applies if the HAP is abated.\n\nPREPAYMENT (5-6.F). If the owner prepays while under a Watch List contract, the AE notifies Recap, the project is removed from the list, and the AE issues a Basic Renewal Contract at the comparable market rents established under the Watch List contract.\n\nRecall the Chapter 2 rule (2-3.C.2.a): Watch List projects renew only under Option Three for three consecutive one-year renewals; after the third, if improvement is satisfactory, any eligible option.\n\nWorked example — OCAF timing. Hawthorne's first Watch List contract (Jan 1–Dec 31) sets rents at $905. No adjustment during the year. The second contract starts January 1 with an OCAF of 3.0% → $932.15, fixed for that year. The third starts with an OCAF of 2.4% → $954.52. At the end of year three, the Account Resolution Specialist finds no deterioration → removed; the owner may choose any eligible option."},
  {"heading":"Reading a Recap File: A Consultant's Walkthrough","body":"When a new client hands you a property that has been to Recap, the history determines the future. Work through it in this order.\n\n1. WHAT HAPPENED AT RECAP?\n• No referral (exempt, or at market) → general rules apply.\n• Lite → an ordinary 524 contract since; at subsequent renewal, any eligible option (5-5.A). But it remains \"once eligible\" for a Full before the Sunset (5-1.B, 5-3.B.2).\n• Full → locked into Option Three for the Use Agreement's life. Find the closing date, the Use Agreement term, and whether exception rents apply.\n• Watch List → one-year contracts, three years, removal conditions.\n• Opted out or prepaid during Recap → what Recap determined as market; and if prepaid, NO Option Four (5-4.E).\n\n2. WHAT DOES THE USE AGREEMENT SAY?\n• Term (at least 30 years); any extensions (5-5.C); exception rents (5-5.D).\n\n3. WHERE ARE WE IN THE CONTRACT CYCLE?\n• Expiration; OCAF anniversary; whether early termination for a refinancing is in play.\n\n4. WHAT DOES THE OWNER WANT?\n• Higher rents → a Full M2M owner cannot get them from an RCS or budget; only OCAF. A Lite owner may be able to (MUTM, Option Two budget).\n• A refinancing → Full: terminate-and-renew with possible Use Agreement extension; exception rents capped at 30 years total.\n• To leave → a Full owner must accept HUD's renewal offers during the Use Agreement; a Watch List owner can opt out (a removal ground); a Lite owner may opt out at expiration subject to Chapter 11 notice rules.\n\nCASE 1. Sequoia Terrace: Full M2M closed 2006, 30-year Use Agreement to 2036, first contract 2006–2026. In 2026 HUD offers a 10-year renewal, OCAF only. The owner's broker says rents could be 40% higher at market and suggests MUTM. Your answer: Option Three B is the only option; the owner must accept the offer (5-5.B); no RCS is allowed; no MUTM during the Use Agreement. Plan for 2036, when the Use Agreement ends and any eligible option opens (5-5.E).\n\nCASE 2. Magnolia Court: Lite in 2004 (rents cut to market), renewed since under Option Two. Rents are now 8% below market; REAC 81; MOR Satisfactory; for-profit owner. Options: MUTM if it clears 3-2 and 3-3 or 3-6; or, before the Sunset, a request to enter Full M2M under 5-3.B.2 if the debt has become a problem. Lite history does not lock it in.\n\nCASE 3. Cottonwood Village: Watch List contract, year 2 of 3. Owner wants to prepay its FHA loan and leave HUD's mortgage programs. On prepayment, Recap is notified, the project leaves the Watch List, and the AE issues a Basic Renewal Contract at the comparable market rents set under the Watch List (5-6.F). The owner should not expect Option Four afterwards if the prepayment happens while the project is being processed at Recap (5-4.E).\n\nCASE 4. Dogwood Place: Full M2M with exception rents, restructured 2001. In 2026 (year 25) the lender for a refinancing wants a 10-year contract. 25 + 10 = 35 > 30 → not permitted (5-5.D). The most HUD can do is 5 years (to year 30). The refinancing must be sized to a 5-year HAP term or wait."},
  ]
},
{
  "id":"r9","unit":"ur3","number":9,
  "title":"Option Six: Opt-Outs & Owner Notification",
  "subtitle":"Processing an opt-out, the one-year notice, what makes a notice acceptable, short-term contracts, and the 120-day confirmation.",
  "sections":[
  {"heading":"The Opt-Out Option and HUD's Last Chance","body":"Option Six (Chapter 8 of the Guide) is the owner's choice not to renew. HUD is committed to preserving affordable housing, and the Regional Office informs owners of their options — but an owner who satisfies the requirements may opt out, by submitting HUD-9624 and electing Option Six (8-1). In doing so the owner must honor the tenants' right to remain and meet the notification requirements.\n\nTWO TIMING RULES THAT CUT IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS (8-1).\n• HUD will renew up until the DAY the contract expires if the owner changes its mind.\n• HUD has NO AUTHORITY to enter into a new contract after the owner has opted out.\nThe owner's window to reverse an opt-out closes when the contract expires. After that, the project-based subsidy is gone.\n\nTHE ONE-YEAR NOTICE (8-2). The owner must provide one-year notification of its intent to opt out to BOTH the tenants AND the AE/CA (Appendix 11-1 has the sample letter).\n\nAE/CA REVIEW (8-3.A). The review should happen in time to obtain tenant-based assistance and allow tenants adequate search time. The AE/CA should:\n1. determine that the owner elected Option Six;\n2. confirm all documentation, including HUD-9624;\n3. confirm eligibility to opt out:\n   a. Is there a restriction in the HAP contract or a Use Agreement that prohibits it? (Demo projects cannot opt out during the Demo Use Agreement; Preservation POAs generally prohibit it; Full M2M owners must accept renewal offers — Chapters 6 and 8 of this course.)\n   b. Did the owner give acceptable one-year notice to tenants AND the AE/CA? Does the letter state that the owner will honor the right to remain?\n   c. Does the owner certify it will honor each tenant's right to remain as long as the project is offered as rental housing (if the PHA approves the rent as reasonable — equal to the new rent charged), unless there are grounds for eviction under State or local law?\n4. log the request in iREMS.\n\nTHE 120-DAY CONTACT (8-3.B). Four months (120 days) before expiration, upon receipt of the owner's written notice, the AE should again contact the owner to explore alternatives. Chapter 11 of the Guide (11-4.G) puts it more pointedly: explore alternatives to opting out, PARTICULARLY MUTM — and HUD will renew up to the day the contract expires.\n\nPIH COORDINATION (8-3.C). The AE coordinates with PIH staff in the Regional Center/Satellite Office. If tenant-based assistance is delayed, the CA and owner may enter a short-term contract of up to one year at CURRENT contract rents, using HUD-9636. PIH Notice 2001-41(HA) is the conversion guidance.\n\nRECAP PROJECTS (8-3.D). Opt-outs during Recap processing follow 5-4.D (Chapter 7 of this course).\n\nWorked example — the last day. Juniper Gardens' contract expires March 31. The owner gave a proper notice the previous March. On March 20 the owner tells the AE it has reconsidered after an MUTM analysis showed market-level rents. HUD can still renew — up until March 31. On April 2 it cannot."},
  {"heading":"Improper Notice: What Happens When the Clock Wasn't Started","body":"An owner that gets the notice wrong does not escape the one-year requirement; it pays for it.\n\nCORRECTIVE NOTICE (8-3.A.3.b note). If the notice was improper, the owner must give an acceptable one-year written notice to the tenants and the AE/CA. During that period:\n1. the family's contribution to rent can be increased ONLY as needed for changes in household income and/or from annual recertification during the period required to fulfill the full notification time frame;\n2. the AE/CA offers the owner a short-term contract (rent set under 2-8).\n   a. The owner renews, the contract ends mid-month, and it submitted a voucher for the full month → nothing further.\n   b. The owner does NOT renew, the contract ends mid-month, and it submitted a voucher for the full month → the owner pays back the funds for the period not covered; the CA determines the amount and HUD processes the recovery.\n\nUNACCEPTABLE NOTICE — THE TENANT SIDE (11-4.E). If the owner fails to give proper one-year notice to HUD/CA and the tenants, the owner must permit the tenants to REMAIN in their units WITHOUT increasing their portion of the rent for whatever period is needed to meet all the notification requirements.\n1. Section 524(d)(1) of MAHRA requires HUD to make enhanced voucher assistance available to low-income families residing in an assisted unit in a project of MORE THAN FOUR dwelling units upon HAP expiration — with NO exception for a missing or defective notice.\n2. The PHA still decides family eligibility for enhanced voucher assistance:\n   a. eligible and moving → the PHA immediately provides a voucher (the special enhanced provisions do not apply when the family uses the voucher to move);\n   b. eligible and staying → the enhanced-voucher tenancy commences when the owner satisfies the one-year notice; until then the family stays with no increase in its portion of the rent, so there is no need for the enhanced voucher to begin sooner;\n   c. not eligible → the family may remain with no rent increase until the owner satisfies the notice period.\n3. Owners are encouraged to enter a short-term renewal at current rents long enough to give the full one-year notice. Otherwise, the owner receives ONLY THE TENANT PORTION of the rent the families were paying under the expired contract until the full one-year notice period is met — because the enhanced-voucher tenancy does not commence until then.\n\nWorked example — the cost of a bad notice. Ash Court (60 units, all assisted) let its contract expire June 30 after a notice the AE/CA found defective. A corrected notice went out July 15. The owner declined a short-term renewal. Average contract rent $1,150; average tenant payment $340. From July 1 until the following July 15, the owner collects only the tenant portion: about $340 × 60 = $20,400 a month instead of $69,000 — roughly $48,600 a month lost for more than a year. A short-term renewal at current rents would have avoided it.\n\nTHE LESSON. An opt-out is a one-year project that starts with a correct letter. Have the AE/CA review the letter BEFORE it goes out."},
  {"heading":"The One-Year Notice: Format and Content","body":"THE STATUTE (11-4.A). Section 8(c)(8) of the U.S. Housing Act requires a one-year written notice to tenants AND HUD before a contract terminates or expires, stating the owner's intentions — renew or not.\nNote: one-year notice is NOT required when the owner is terminating early to renew for 20 years or for the remaining life of a use agreement.\n\nFORMAT (11-4.B).\n1. On the owner's (or authorized representative's) letterhead, signed.\n2. Served by delivery directly to each unit, or mailed to each tenant (head of household). TAPING the notice to the outside of the unit is NOT acceptable.\n3. In other languages as appropriate; the translation cost is an eligible project expense.\n\nCONTENT (11-4.C). The sample opt-out letter is Appendix 11-1.\n1. The notice must contain the sample language.\n2. The owner is not required to state reasons, but is encouraged to.\n3. It must state that the owner will honor the right to remain and continually renew leases if: the project is offered as rental housing; the PHA continues to find the rent reasonable; and there is no cause for eviction.\n4. An owner that said it would renew and then decides to opt out must give a NEW one-year notice to tenants, HUD and the CA.\n5. An owner that elects Recap must give a new one-year notice to tenants upon execution of an Interim-Lite or Interim-Full contract.\n6. An owner at Recap that decides to opt out during restructuring must give a 120-DAY notice (Appendix 11-3) IN ADDITION to the one-year notice issued on entry to Recap.\n\nTHE SAMPLE LETTERS.\n• Appendix 11-1 — \"Owner Does Not Intend to Renew.\" Its key features: the contract's expiration date (one year from the letter); a bold statement that the owner does not intend to renew; that, if Congress makes funds available, HUD will provide eligible tenants with tenant-based assistance; that the owner will honor the right to remain while the property is offered as rental housing, absent cause for eviction; that tenants may move with a voucher; that project-based assistance continues for one year and the owner may still agree to renew; and that approximately four months (120 days) before expiration, the owner must confirm its final decision, after which the PHA will contact tenants — and tenants should not move before consulting the PHA.\n• Appendix 11-2 — \"Owner Intends to Renew.\" States that, if Congress makes funds available, the owner will renew; that if it later decides not to, tenants will receive at least one year of advance notice.\n• Appendix 11-3 — Recap second notice of opt-out (120 days), confirming the earlier one-year letter.\n\nOTHER RULES.\n• 11-4.I: State and local notice requirements also apply.\n• 11-4.J: owners renewing for a long term are encouraged to tell tenants so — and that they will receive one-year notice before it expires.\n• 11-5: reasonable efforts for persons with Limited English Proficiency (Executive Order 13166; HUD's final LEP guidance of January 22, 2007).\n• 11-6: effective communication for persons with disabilities under 24 CFR 8.6 — TTY, audio tape, Braille.\n\nWorked example — spot the defects. A notice is printed on plain paper, unsigned, taped to each door, in English only in a building where a third of households speak Spanish, and omits the right-to-remain statement. Four defects: no letterhead/signature (B.1), taping (B.2), no translation where appropriate (B.3), missing required content (C.3). The one-year clock has not started."},
  {"heading":"AE/CA Review of the Notice and Short-Term Contracts","body":"REVIEW (11-4.D). All tenant notification letters must be reviewed by the AE/CA, either before or at the time they are sent. Where HUD is not the CA, the owner sends a copy to HUD.\n• HUD does not require pre-issuance review, but owners are ENCOURAGED to submit letters 30 days in advance of the one-year period, to avoid having to correct a faulty notice after tenants have it.\n• Absent early submission, the owner must send the letters to the AE/CA and the tenants AT THE SAME TIME.\n• \"Intend to renew\" letters: no AE/CA action beyond reviewing them for the requirements.\n• \"Does not intend to renew\" letters: the AE/CA must review within 30 DAYS of receipt. No HUD approval is required before the one-year clock starts; but if the review finds the letter noncompliant, the owner is told a corrected notice must be issued — and the one-year clock DOES NOT BEGIN until the proper notice is provided to HUD, the CA and the tenants.\n1. Acceptable → no AE/CA action needed.\n2. Unacceptable → the AE/CA notifies the owner that it has failed to provide proper notification.\n\nWorked example — when the clock starts. An owner mails a non-renewal letter to tenants and the CA on April 1. On April 24 the CA finds it omits the right-to-remain language. The owner issues a corrected letter on May 10. The one-year clock runs from May 10, not April 1. If the contract expires the following April 30, the owner is about 10 days short — which is why a short-term renewal is so often needed.\n\nSHORT-TERM CONTRACTS AND NEW NOTICES (11-4.F). In general, upon executing a short-term contract, the owner must give a one-year notice to tenants and HUD/CA. Over that one-year period, the owner and HUD/CA may agree to additional short-term extensions WITHOUT a new notice each time — so long as each extension falls WITHIN the one-year time frame of the notice. A short-term renewal accepted AFTER the 12-month notice period has expired triggers ANOTHER 12-month notice requirement. Exceptions:\n1. The owner has already fulfilled its notice requirement and agrees to a contract of less than a year SOLELY to give HUD time to provide tenant-based assistance → no new notice; the contract protects the tenants.\n2. The owner gave proper notice of intent to opt out and then accepts a short-term renewal to consider MUTM → no new notice.\n\nTHE 120-DAY DECISION (11-4.G). One hundred twenty days before expiration, owners must notify HUD's Regional Center/Satellite Office Director or CA in writing whether they will renew or opt out. In an opt-out, HUD needs that time to obtain enhanced vouchers. The AE/CA should again explore alternatives — particularly MUTM. HUD will renew up until the day the contract expires.\n\nPutting the timeline together for a planned opt-out with a June 30 expiration:\n• Prior May 31: send the letter to the AE/CA for review (30 days before the one-year period — encouraged).\n• Prior June 30: serve the one-year notice on tenants and the AE/CA.\n• Within 30 days: AE/CA review of the non-renewal letter.\n• March 2 (120 days before June 30): written confirmation of the final decision; AE/CA contact about alternatives; voucher ordering begins.\n• June 30: last day HUD can renew."},
  {"heading":"Opt-Out Strategy: Advising the Owner Who Wants Out","body":"Owners rarely decide to opt out in a vacuum. A good consultant runs the alternatives and the costs before the letter goes out.\n\nSTEP 1 — CAN THEY? Check for anything that forbids it:\n• Demo Use Agreement (no opt-out — 7-2.A).\n• Preservation POA and Use Agreement (generally prohibited; Headquarters approval if allowed — 7-5.C).\n• Full M2M Use Agreement (must accept HUD's renewal offers — 5-5.B).\n• Any other recorded use agreement or regulatory restriction tied to financing (HFA bonds, LIHTC, HOME).\nAn opt-out does not release the owner from affordability covenants in other documents.\n\nSTEP 2 — WHAT ARE THEY GIVING UP?\n• Project-based subsidy on every assisted unit.\n• Under enhanced vouchers, tenants can remain, and the owner must keep renewing leases absent cause (Chapter 10 of this course). The owner will be leasing to the same households, paid through the PHA at a rent the PHA finds reasonable.\n• Exposure to a new set of rules (HCV program, HQS/NSPIRE-V inspections by the PHA, PHA rent reasonableness).\n\nSTEP 3 — WHAT ARE THE ALTERNATIVES?\n• MUTM (Option One) if rents are below market — 11-4.G tells the AE/CA to raise it specifically.\n• Option Two with a 20-year contract and increased distributions (for new regulation owners).\n• A Chapter 15 Transfer to a preservation buyer (Chapter 16 of this course).\n\nSTEP 4 — IF STILL OPTING OUT, RUN IT CLEANLY.\n• Use the Appendix 11-1 language verbatim; letterhead; signed; delivered or mailed; translated where needed.\n• Submit to the AE/CA 30 days early.\n• Calendar the 120-day confirmation.\n• If the contract expires before a full year can run, take the short-term renewal at current rents.\n• Remember: no early termination to opt out (2-4.E). An owner cannot cut its contract short to leave sooner.\n\nCASE. Bayview Terrace: for-profit owner, Section 8 New Construction, 96 units, contract expiring in 14 months, FHA loan paid off. Comparable market rents are 35% above contract rents. The owner wants out to convert to market rate.\n• Restrictions: the regulatory agreement ended with the mortgage; no other use agreement → may opt out.\n• Alternative: MUTM. If the project clears 3-2 (MOR, REAC, FASS) and 3-3, MUTM brings rents to market — capped at 150% of FMR for One-A unless a One-B criterion is met. If 52% of units are elderly, rents go to full market.\n• If the owner still opts out: tenants receiving enhanced vouchers may remain; the owner must renew their leases while the property is rental housing and the PHA finds the rent reasonable. Converting to market rate means market-rate rents for the enhanced-voucher households, paid largely through the PHA — not an empty building.\n• Timing: send the one-year notice now (14 months out), with AE/CA review first.\nMost owners in Bayview's position, once they see that MUTM produces market rents and that opting out does not clear the building, renew."},
  ]
},
{
  "id":"r10","unit":"ur3","number":10,
  "title":"Tenant Protection: Vouchers & the Right to Remain",
  "subtitle":"Regular vs enhanced vouchers, housing conversion actions, eligibility, the right to remain, and vouchers after HUD refuses to renew.",
  "sections":[
  {"heading":"Regular Housing Choice Vouchers","body":"Chapter 11 of the Guide begins with definitions, because the renewal decision determines which kind of voucher tenants receive.\n\nREGULAR HOUSING CHOICE VOUCHER (11-1.A). Section 8 tenant-based assistance provided to eligible families and individuals to obtain affordable housing in the private market.\n1. The voucher belongs to the family or individual and generally allows the holder to choose any housing that meets program requirements.\n2. Local Public Housing Agencies (PHAs) administer vouchers. The PHA establishes the payment standard, determines eligibility, certifies income and calculates the tenant's share. The payment standard is based on the area's Fair Market Rents (FMRs).\n3. The payment standard is the MAXIMUM subsidy. The monthly housing assistance payment (HAP) is the LESSER of:\n   • the PHA payment standard MINUS the family's Total Tenant Payment (TTP), or\n   • the gross rent of the unit MINUS the TTP.\n\nTHE FAMILY'S TTP is the GREATEST of:\n• 30 percent of adjusted monthly income;\n• 10 percent of gross monthly income;\n• the welfare rent, in as-paid states; or\n• the PHA minimum rent ($0–$50).\nIf the gross rent exceeds the payment standard, the family pays the difference out of pocket, IN ADDITION to the TTP.\n\nWorked example 1. Payment standard $1,300. Unit gross rent $1,250. Adjusted monthly income $1,500; gross monthly income $1,800; minimum rent $50.\nTTP = greatest of 30% × $1,500 = $450; 10% × $1,800 = $180; $50 → $450.\nHAP = lesser of ($1,300 − $450 = $850) or ($1,250 − $450 = $800) = $800.\nFamily pays $1,250 − $800 = $450.\n\nWorked example 2 — gross rent above payment standard. Same family; unit gross rent $1,420.\nHAP = lesser of ($1,300 − $450 = $850) or ($1,420 − $450 = $970) = $850.\nFamily pays $1,420 − $850 = $570 — the $450 TTP plus the $120 by which gross rent exceeds the payment standard.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS IN A RENEWAL CONTEXT. When a project-based contract ends, the family's rent no longer comes from the project's contract rent. With a REGULAR voucher, the subsidy is capped at the area's FMR-based payment standard. If the project's rent is above that standard, the family pays the difference or moves. That is precisely the problem enhanced vouchers solve."},
  {"heading":"Enhanced Vouchers and Housing Conversion Actions","body":"ENHANCED VOUCHER (11-1.B). Tenant-based assistance for eligible families affected by certain housing conversion actions — called \"eligibility events.\"\n1. THE ENHANCEMENT. The subsidy is \"enhanced\" to cover the difference between the normally applicable payment standard and the possibly higher rent of the unit going through the conversion. The payment standard for an enhanced voucher is the GROSS RENT OF THE UNIT, provided the PHA determines the gross rent is reasonable in comparison to similar unassisted units in the market area.\n2. THE SPECIAL MINIMUM RENT. The family must continue paying at least the same amount it was paying on the date of the conversion action — unless its gross family income DECREASES by at least 15 PERCENT from the date of the eligibility event (PIH Notice 2001-41(HA), Part II C(3)(c)).\n3. TIED TO THE PROJECT. The enhancement exists only while the family stays. If the family moves, the voucher becomes a REGULAR voucher, subject to the regular payment standard.\n\nWorked example 1 — the enhancement. A project opts out; post-conversion gross rent for a family's unit is $1,620, which the PHA finds reasonable. The area payment standard is $1,300. Family TTP is $410; on the eligibility event date the family was paying $395.\nPayment standard (enhanced) = $1,620. HAP = $1,620 − TTP. The family's payment must be at least $395 (special minimum) — its $410 TTP exceeds that, so it pays $410; HAP = $1,210.\nWith a regular voucher, HAP would have been $1,300 − $410 = $890, leaving the family paying $730.\n\nWorked example 2 — the 15% test. At the eligibility event the family paid $520 and its gross income was $2,400 a month. A year later its gross income falls to $2,100 (a 12.5% drop). The special minimum still applies — it pays at least $520 even if 30% of adjusted income would now be lower. Had income fallen to $2,000 (a 16.7% drop), the special minimum would no longer apply.\n\nHOUSING CONVERSION ACTIONS (11-1.C). Some conversion actions are \"eligibility events\" requiring HUD to offer ENHANCED vouchers; others result in REGULAR vouchers. Conversion actions include:\n• a project-based Section 8 OPT-OUT;\n• a preservation PREPAYMENT;\n• HUD ENFORCEMENT actions; and\n• HUD PROPERTY DISPOSITION (PD).\nNotice PIH 2001-41(HA), November 14, 2001, is the governing guidance.\n\nWHICH VOUCHER? (11-2).\nA. OPT-OUTS — enhanced.\n  1. Notice to tenants AND the CA at least one year before termination; request and certification to the local HUD office/CA not less than 120 days before expiration.\n  2. Section 524(d) of MAHRA: subject to appropriations, HUD makes enhanced vouchers available to low-income families living in an assisted unit on the date of HAP expiration.\n  3. HUD Handbook 1378 (relocation) — Chapter 3 addresses persons with disabilities.\nB. HUD ENFORCEMENT ACTIONS (with or without PD) — regular. HUD terminates or does not offer renewal because of the owner's failure to comply — material adverse financial or managerial actions (13-1.B) resulting in a monetary or technical default under an FHA-insured mortgage, or a documented material violation of the Regulatory Agreement. REGULAR vouchers are normally provided, because the project's condition won't allow tenants to remain.\n\nThe logic: an opt-out is the owner's business decision about a habitable property, so the law lets tenants stay with enhanced subsidy. An enforcement action usually means the property is not a place tenants should stay."},
  {"heading":"The Right to Remain","body":"WHY IT MATTERS (11-3.A). Contract expirations cause worry and fear among tenants; they need clear information.\n\nTHE RIGHT (11-3.B). Enhanced voucher holders may REMAIN in their units as long as the units are offered as rental housing. The voucher will pay the rent if it is reasonable. The owner cannot terminate the tenancy except for cause under Federal, State or local law. To receive the full rent, the owner must enter a contract with the PHA; an owner who refuses is subject to remedies under 42 U.S.C. 1437f(t)(1) and State or local law.\n\nTHE STATUTE.\n1. Public Law 106-246, Section 2801 (July 13, 2000) amended Section 8(t) of the U.S. Housing Act: \"...the assisted family may elect to remain in the same project in which the family was residing on the date of the eligibility event for the project...\"\n2. The right continues while the unit is offered as rental housing, absent good cause, and while the PHA finds the rent reasonable. Owners must CONTINUALLY RENEW leases.\n\nTHE OWNER'S CERTIFICATION. The same commitment appears in three places an owner signs or sends:\n• the opt-out certification reviewed under 8-3.A.3.c;\n• the one-year notice content required by 11-4.C.3; and\n• the Appendix 11-1 sample letter (\"we will honor your right as a tenant to remain at the property on this basis as long as it continues to be offered as rental housing, provided that there is no cause for eviction\").\n\nWHAT THE RIGHT DOES — AND DOES NOT — DO.\n• It does not freeze the rent. The owner may set a new rent; the enhanced voucher pays it if the PHA finds it reasonable compared with similar unassisted units.\n• It does not require the owner to keep the building as rental housing forever. If the owner converts to condominiums or another non-rental use, the condition \"offered as rental housing\" ends.\n• It does not protect a tenant who gives cause for eviction under applicable law.\n• It does not survive a move. A family that moves takes a REGULAR voucher.\n\nWorked example. After Bayview Terrace opts out, the owner sets two-bedroom rents at $2,150. The PHA's rent reasonableness review finds comparable unassisted units at $2,050–$2,200 → reasonable. Enhanced-voucher families stay; the PHA pays $2,150 minus each family's payment. The owner may not refuse to renew a family's lease merely because it would prefer a market-rate tenant. If the owner refused to contract with the PHA, it would face remedies under 1437f(t)(1).\n\nNow suppose the owner sets rents at $2,600 and the PHA finds them unreasonable. The enhanced payment standard is the gross rent only if reasonable — the PHA will not approve $2,600. The owner either accepts a reasonable rent for those units or the families, unable to pay the difference, may have to move with regular vouchers. Rent reasonableness is the real limit on post-opt-out rents for enhanced-voucher households."},
  {"heading":"Who Is Eligible for Tenant-Based Assistance","body":"Eligibility for a voucher is NOT automatic (11-3.C). Being income eligible does not by itself make a family eligible.\n\nTHE PHA SCREENS (11-3.C.1).\na. The PHA recertifies and screens families, and may deny assistance under 24 CFR 982.552 and 982.553. PHAs are encouraged to use maximum flexibility — for example, repayment agreements. A family denied assistance must be promptly notified, with a brief statement of reasons, and offered an informal review under 982.554.\nb. If the owner's income certification is no more than SIX MONTHS old and acceptable (based on a small sampling), the PHA may use it rather than re-verifying from scratch.\n\nWHO IS ELIGIBLE IN AN OPT-OUT (11-3.C.2). In a regular opt-out — one NOT preceded by a prepayment — families must be LOW-INCOME (including very low-income) and residing in a unit covered by the expiring contract. That includes families who moved into a vacated unit DURING the one-year notice period.\na. Unlike a preservation prepayment, a family ABOVE 80 percent of area median income is NOT eligible because of an opt-out.\nb. Preservation prepayments are governed by Notice 2004-17.\n\nNEW TENANTS DURING THE NOTICE YEAR (11-3.C.3). Owners are encouraged to include a lease addendum for anyone moving in during the one-year period:\n(1) \"If the HAP contract terminates for any reason, the lease terminates automatically\"; and\n(2) \"tenants who move into a vacated unit during the one-year notification period are not entitled to one-year notice of contract expiration or termination.\"\nNote the interplay: such a family is still potentially ELIGIBLE for an enhanced voucher if low-income (C.2) — the addendum addresses lease termination and notice, not voucher eligibility.\n\nINCOME TARGETING (11-3.C.4). These families are NOT subject to the income targeting requirements of the tenant-based program.\n\nPROCESSING DELAYS (11-3.D). If voucher processing is delayed, HUD must ask the owner to consider a short-term renewal.\n\nWorked examples — eligibility in a regular opt-out:\n• Household at 62% AMI in an assisted unit on the expiration date → low-income → potentially eligible (subject to PHA screening).\n• Household at 84% AMI in an assisted unit → above 80% → NOT eligible due to an opt-out.\n• Household at 45% AMI that moved into a vacated assisted unit four months into the notice year → eligible; the lease addendum may terminate its lease automatically when the HAP ends, but it can still receive an enhanced voucher and elect to remain.\n• Household in an UNASSISTED unit at 60% AMI → not residing in a unit covered by the expiring contract → not eligible through the opt-out.\n\nCONSULTANT'S NOTE. Ask the PHA early whether it will accept the owner's income certifications under C.1.b. Certifications more than six months old at the time of conversion force full re-verification for every household, which is where delays — and short-term renewals — come from."},
  {"heading":"When HUD Refuses to Renew: Regular Vouchers and Relocation","body":"An owner-driven opt-out and a HUD-driven non-renewal both end the project-based contract, but tenants experience them very differently.\n\nHUD'S TENANT NOTICE (13-4).\nA. When HUD elects not to renew for any of the reasons in Section 13-1 (Chapter 14 of this course), the Regional Center or Satellite Office must notify, in writing, ALL tenants receiving Section 8 assistance of HUD's decision.\nB. Section 516(d) of MAHRA: subject to the availability of appropriations, Section 8 tenants and any other low-income tenants residing in the project are provided REGULAR Housing Choice Vouchers and REASONABLE MOVING EXPENSES, as determined by the Secretary.\n\nTHE VOUCHER PROCESS (13-2.B, 13-3.I). The AE/CA immediately begins the process for regular HCVs under Notice PIH 2001-41(HA) — allowing up to 120 days for issuance — and monitors processing with the PHA, PIH and the owner until vouchers are issued. The contract is TERMINATED only AFTER vouchers have been issued to eligible residents. Where the contract is abated and HUD will relocate tenants, the Regional Center/PC Director must obtain authority for a relocation program from the Director of OAMPO, and the AE alerts the Property Disposition Center (13-2.B.3).\n\nOPT-OUT vs REFUSAL TO RENEW\n• Who decides: the owner (Option Six) vs HUD (Sections 516 and 524(a)(2)).\n• Voucher: ENHANCED vs REGULAR.\n• Right to remain: YES, while offered as rental housing and the rent is reasonable vs NO enhanced right — the premise is that the property should not be occupied as is.\n• Payment standard: unit's reasonable gross rent vs the PHA's normal payment standard.\n• Moving expenses: not mentioned vs reasonable moving expenses (516(d)).\n• Who is covered: low-income families in assisted units (incl. those moving in during the notice year) vs Section 8 tenants AND other low-income tenants residing in the project.\n• Notice: owner's one-year notice vs HUD's written notice to tenants.\n\nWorked example. HUD refuses to renew Cedar Ridge after repeated failing REAC scores and uncorrected EH&S deficiencies. The Regional Center notifies all assisted tenants in writing. The AE/CA starts regular voucher processing; the PHA has up to 120 days. Low-income tenants in the project's UNASSISTED units are also eligible for regular vouchers and reasonable moving expenses under 516(d). No family has a statutory right to remain with an enhanced voucher. When vouchers are issued, the contract is terminated; if possible, the budget authority moves to another project through the 8(bb) process (Housing Notice 2014-14).\n\nTHE CONSULTANT'S TAKEAWAY. Tenant protection is not one rule; it depends on WHY the contract ended. Before advising on a contract's end, identify which category it falls into: opt-out, preservation prepayment, enforcement action, or property disposition. The answer controls the voucher type, the right to remain, who qualifies, and how long the process takes."},
  ]
},
];

