const CHAPTERS_E = [
{
  "id":"r14","unit":"ur5","number":14,
  "title":"Physical Condition & HUD's Refusal to Renew",
  "subtitle":"How REAC scores and EH&S deficiencies drive renewal decisions, the grounds for refusal, the non-renewal process, and the owner's appeal.",
  "sections":[
  {"heading":"Physical Condition as a Renewal Factor","body":"THE PRINCIPLE (12-1). A project's physical condition — and its HISTORY of physical condition — are important components of the decision to renew.\n\nTHE INSPECTION. REAC, or the mortgagee, inspects using the REAC physical inspection protocol every ONE TO THREE YEARS, depending on the project's last score.\n\nTHE POSSIBLE OUTCOMES. Depending on condition, a contract may be RENEWED, ABATED, TERMINATED, or ALLOWED TO EXPIRE. Which course the AE takes on an owner's renewal request depends on the inspection score(s) and on whether the owner corrects — and certifies that it has corrected — all deficiencies in the report AND identified in the owner's own survey of the project, acceptably and on time.\n\nTHE AE'S THRESHOLD DUTY (12-2). An EH&S deficiency notice or a REAC report may affect the decision to renew, abate or terminate. The AE must determine whether the project is following HUD's physical condition standards BEFORE processing a renewal request. HUD usually will NOT abate a contract based on failing REAC scores unless OAMPO at Headquarters approves.\n\nNOTICE H 2015-02. Issued March 2, 2015 (\"Required Actions for Multifamily Housing Projects Receiving Failing Scores from HUD's Real Estate Assessment Center\"), superseding Notice H 2012-16 on flagging in the Active Partners Performance System (APPS). It implements Section 230 of the Consolidated Appropriations Act of 2014 and Section 226 of the FY 2015 Appropriations Act, which require HUD to take certain steps when a project scores 59 OR BELOW.\n\nACCESSIBILITY. Owners must comply with physical accessibility requirements — ADA Titles II and III, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, the Architectural Barriers Act (1968), the Fair Housing Act, and state or local laws. Certain accessibility barriers may themselves be EH&S deficiencies: accessible routes from a unit to transportation or parking blocked by broken elevators or automatic doors; sidewalks obstructed by dumpsters; curb cuts or sidewalks in disrepair. These are often visible during an inspection, and the result should be referred to FHEO.\n\nTHE STANDARDS THEMSELVES. The Guide points to the December 8, 2000 Federal Register publication of the physical inspection standards and the August 9, 2012 Final Notice on scoring and the revised dictionary of deficiency definitions. (HUD staff guidance on the oversight protocol is on an internal site; others should contact the Regional Center/Satellite Office.)\nSource note: the Guide's physical-condition references predate HUD's transition from the UPCS/REAC protocol to NSPIRE (the National Standards for the Physical Inspection of Real Estate). The renewal logic — scores, EH&S correction, certification, and the 60-point line — is what this chapter teaches; for the current inspection standard itself, see the NSPIRE Academy.\n\nWHY CONDITION COMES UP AGAIN AND AGAIN IN THIS COURSE. The 60-point line recurs:\n• MUTM eligibility: REAC 60 or higher, no uncorrected EH&S (3-2.B).\n• Increased distributions: REAC 60 or higher, no uncorrected EH&S (2-12.E).\n• Chapter 15 Transfers without repairs: REAC score GREATER than 60, no uncorrected EHS, not troubled (15-16).\n• Non-renewal under 13-2: EH&S not corrected and re-inspection score below 60.\nA single inspection can therefore open or close several doors at once. Before any renewal strategy session, pull the last three REAC reports."},
  {"heading":"EH&S Deficiencies and the Score-Based Response","body":"EXIGENT HEALTH AND SAFETY (12-2.A). A REAC inspector may issue an EH&S citation when serious deficiencies must be corrected IMMEDIATELY.\n\nTHE OWNER MUST:\na. IMMEDIATELY correct or mitigate EH&S deficiencies — regardless of the project's score; and\nb. submit a written certification to the AE within 3 BUSINESS DAYS of the inspection date certifying that ALL EH&S deficiencies at the project have been corrected or mitigated — not just those cited in the EH&S notification.\n\nThat word ALL matters. REAC inspects a sample of units. If the inspector found a blocked egress in unit 204, the owner's certification covers every blocked egress anywhere at the property.\n\nTHE AE MUST:\na. NOT abate a contract solely because an EH&S notice was issued, but consider what the owner did to correct all EH&S deficiencies;\nb. FLAG the owner in APPS (the 2530 system) if the owner does not correct or mitigate all EH&S deficiencies; and\nc. make an elective referral to the Departmental Enforcement Center (DEC) or the Office of General Counsel if the owner is uncooperative and refuses to follow the protocol.\n\nTHE PHYSICAL INSPECTION REPORT (12-2.B).\n1. The owner receives the report via the internet. When the score is BELOW 60, the owner must survey the ENTIRE project to find similar problems in other units, based on REAC's findings.\n2. The AE/CA (which sees the report in iREMS) must:\n   a. use the report, the owner's survey, the owner's responses, and any later REAC reports to assess condition when deciding on renewal;\n   b. review the iREMS administrative record together with recent inspections — considering the quality of management and the owner's ability to meet all requirements. A CHANGE OF MANAGEMENT may be a condition placed on the owner before renewal.\n3. SCORE ABOVE 60:\n   a. renew if the owner corrected or mitigated the EH&S deficiencies (unless the citations were appealed);\n   b. follow Chapter 13 if the owner has not (unless appealed).\n4. SCORE BELOW 60: the AE follows Notice H 2015-02 (or current instructions) on the owner's timely submission of the \"Project Owner's Certification\" and the re-inspection results:\n   a. RENEW if the owner is in compliance with current requirements;\n   b. a SHORT-TERM RENEWAL if not, until OAMPO at Headquarters approves a Compliance Disposition and/or Enforcement (CDE) plan or other enforcement action is taken. Note: short-term renewals may be needed where enforcement is anticipated or consummated, and until vouchers can be ordered.\nSource note: 12-2.B speaks of scores \"above 60\" and \"below 60\" and is silent on a score of exactly 60; Notice H 2015-02's trigger is \"59 or below,\" so a 60 falls on the passing side. Section 2-12.E likewise uses \"60 or higher.\"\n\nWorked example 1. Inspection on Tuesday, score 72, three EH&S items cited (a missing smoke detector, an exposed wire, a blocked fire exit). The owner fixes those three, finds and fixes two more exposed wires in units not inspected, and submits its certification Thursday — within 3 business days, certifying ALL EH&S deficiencies are corrected. The AE/CA renews.\n\nWorked example 2. Score 54. The owner surveys the whole project, submits the Project Owner's Certification on time, and the re-inspection scores 68. In compliance → renew.\n\nWorked example 3. Score 49; the owner misses the certification deadline; the re-inspection scores 52. Not in compliance → short-term renewal pending an OAMPO-approved CDE plan or other enforcement. If the plan leads to non-renewal, Chapter 13 applies."},
  {"heading":"When HUD May Refuse to Renew","body":"THE AUTHORITY (13-1). Under Sections 516 and 524(a)(2) of MAHRA:\n\nA. HUD MAY REFUSE TO RENEW if it determines that:\n1. the owner or purchaser engaged in material adverse financial or managerial actions or omissions regarding THIS project;\n2. the owner or purchaser engaged in such actions or omissions regarding OTHER projects of the owner or purchaser that are federally assisted, or financed with a loan from — or a mortgage insured or guaranteed by — a federal agency; or\n3. the owner or purchaser materially failed to follow MAHRA's procedures and requirements after notice and an opportunity to cure.\n\nNote the reach of A.2: misconduct at an owner's OTHER federally assisted or federally financed properties is grounds to refuse renewal here.\n\nB. MATERIAL ADVERSE FINANCIAL OR MANAGERIAL ACTIONS OR OMISSIONS include:\n1. materially violating any Federal, State or local law or regulation regarding the project or any other federally assisted project, after notice and an opportunity to cure — including failing to adhere to a judicial or administrative order to comply with a nondiscrimination or equal opportunity requirement;\n2. materially breaching a Section 8 HAP contract, after notice and an opportunity to cure;\n3. materially violating a regulatory or other business agreement with the Secretary or a participating administrative entity, after notice and an opportunity to cure — including failing to implement a voluntary compliance agreement addressing a nondiscrimination finding, or a conciliation agreement addressing a Fair Housing Act charge;\n4. REPEATEDLY and materially violating any Federal, State or local law or regulation regarding the project or any other federally assisted project;\n5. REPEATEDLY and materially breaching a Section 8 HAP contract;\n6. REPEATEDLY and materially violating any regulatory or other agreement with the Secretary or a participating administrative entity;\n7. REPEATEDLY failing to make mortgage payments at times when project income was SUFFICIENT to maintain and operate the project;\n8. materially failing to maintain the project according to the Uniform Physical Condition Standards (24 CFR Part 5 Subpart G and 24 CFR Part 200 Subpart P), after notice and a reasonable opportunity to cure; or\n9. committing any actions or omissions that would warrant suspension or debarment by the Secretary.\n\nREAD THE STRUCTURE. Items 1–3 and 8 are single material failures that become grounds only AFTER notice and an opportunity to cure. Items 4–7 are REPEATED failures — the repetition itself is the ground, and the text attaches no separate notice-and-cure condition. Item 9 borrows the suspension/debarment standard.\n\nWorked examples — is it a ground?\n• An owner missed two mortgage payments during a year when a hurricane shut down half the building and income fell far short. → Item 7 requires repeated failures when income WAS sufficient. Likely not a ground on these facts.\n• An owner signed a voluntary compliance agreement after a Section 504 finding and never installed the required accessible units, despite HUD's notice and a cure period. → Item 3.\n• An owner's affiliate defaulted repeatedly on an FHA-insured loan at a different property while that property's income covered debt service. → A.2 and B.7 — conduct at OTHER federally financed projects counts.\n• A single failing REAC score with deficiencies corrected within the cure period. → Not item 8: there was no material failure after notice and a reasonable opportunity to cure.\n\nCONNECTION TO RENEWAL PROCESSING. Chapter 3 of this course noted that under 2-17.A.3, non-renewal determinations follow Chapter 13 (for the AE) or the Section 8 Contract Administrators Guide (for a CA), and that where material violations have progressed to HUD deciding to terminate the existing contract, renewal should be DENIED. Suspension or debarment alone does not end the inquiry: HUD may still renew if the project is adequately managed and maintained and its activities were not the cause — documented in iREMS."},
  {"heading":"The Non-Renewal Process","body":"REGIONAL AND HEADQUARTERS ROLES (13-2.A). When a Regional Center or Satellite Office Director decides not to renew based on a REAC physical inspection, the office must follow OAMPO Headquarters guidance before the contract can be abated or allowed to expire.\n\nWHEN OAMPO DETERMINES THE CONTRACT SHOULD NOT BE RENEWED (13-2.B):\n\n1. MORE THAN 120 DAYS remain on the contract — the AE/CA must:\n   a. notify the owner that, because the EH&S deficiencies have not been corrected and the re-inspection score is below 60, a NOTICE OF DEFAULT has been issued and HUD intends to ABATE OR ALLOW THE CONTRACT TO EXPIRE — and, if possible, transfer the project's budget authority to another project through the 8(bb) process (Housing Notice 2014-14);\n   b. IMMEDIATELY begin obtaining REGULAR Housing Choice Vouchers under Notice PIH 2001-41(HA) (or current guidance), allowing up to 120 DAYS for issuance;\n   c. monitor voucher processing with the PHA, PIH and the owner until vouchers are issued.\n\n2. LESS THAN 120 DAYS remain — the AE/CA must:\n   a. send the same notice, stating HUD intends to ABATE the contract (and transfer budget authority via 8(bb) if possible);\n   b. immediately begin obtaining regular HCVs;\n   c. enter a SHORT-TERM RENEWAL at CURRENT rents using the Basic Renewal One Year contract (HUD-9636) — or, if market rents have been determined by an RCS, at rents not exceeding market;\n   d. monitor voucher processing.\n\n3. If the contract will be abated and HUD will RELOCATE tenants, the Regional Center/PC Director must obtain authority for a relocation program from the Director of OAMPO; the AE alerts the Property Disposition Center that a relocation request will go to OAMPO.\n4. Make sure the Regional Center has a written DISPOSITION PLAN in place and shared with the owner BEFORE abating a contract with an underlying insured or Secretary-held mortgage.\n5. TERMINATE the contract AFTER vouchers have been issued to eligible residents, and, if possible, transfer the budget authority via 8(bb).\n6. FLAG the owner in APPS/2530.\n7. DESIGNATE the project as TROUBLED.\n\nAPPEALS (13-2.C) of a non-renewal based on the inspection — or other requests for relief from the score — follow Chapter 13's appeal process (next section).\n\niREMS (13-2.D). The AE records all compliance activities arising from a REAC inspection in iREMS's Workload Management screen: (1) compliance with EH&S and REAC protocols, because it may influence future renewal decisions and HUD needs a complete administrative record for enforcement; (2) all closeout activities, shared with the owner; and (3) a troubled designation in the Risk Management screen.\n\nWHY THE 120-DAY LINE? It matches the voucher timeline: PIH guidance allows up to 120 days to issue vouchers. With more than 120 days left, vouchers can be ready before the contract ends, so HUD can let it expire. With less, a short-term renewal keeps assistance flowing until the vouchers arrive.\n\nWorked example. OAMPO decides on May 15 not to renew Cedar Ridge, whose contract expires July 31 — 77 days away. The AE/CA notifies the owner of the Notice of Default and HUD's intent to abate; starts regular voucher processing; signs a short-term renewal on HUD-9636 at current rents (no RCS-based market determination exists); and monitors vouchers. The Regional Center confirms a written disposition plan is shared with the owner (Cedar Ridge has an FHA-insured mortgage). Vouchers issue September 20; the contract is terminated; the owner is flagged in APPS/2530; the project is designated troubled; and, if possible, the budget authority moves to another project via 8(bb)."},
  {"heading":"The Owner's Appeal of a Refusal to Renew","body":"THE PROCESS (13-3). If HUD refuses to renew, this appeal process applies — and throughout it, HUD takes all steps necessary to protect residents, which may include a short-term contract to complete the appeal.\n\nA. THE AE WILL:\n1. give the owner WRITTEN notice of the reasons for the refusal;\n2. provide a SHORT-TERM RENEWAL pending review of any appeal, if the contract is about to expire; and\n3. give tenants notice of the creation of a CDE (Compliance Disposition and/or Enforcement) Plan.\n\nB. 30 DAYS TO OBJECT OR CURE. The owner has 30 CALENDAR DAYS from receipt of the notice to submit written objections to the Satellite Office Director, or to cure the problems. If it does neither, the decision becomes a FINAL DETERMINATION under Section 516(c) of MAHRA.\n\nC. THE AE'S FINAL DECISION. If the owner objects or asserts it has cured, the AE considers the matter, reviews any action taken, and sends a letter stating the final decision — AFFIRM, MODIFY or REVERSE — with its rationale.\n\nD. 10 DAYS TO APPEAL. Within 10 days of receiving that final decision, the owner may submit a written appeal to the AE contesting it and requesting a CONFERENCE with the Regional Center with jurisdiction.\n\nE. THE CONFERENCE. A Regional Center representative meets the owner at a mutually agreeable time, NO LATER THAN 10 CALENDAR DAYS after the owner requests it.\n\nF. ADDITIONAL INFORMATION. If the owner wants to provide more, a short but mutually agreeable deadline is set.\n\nG. THE REGIONAL CENTER'S DECISION. Within 20 DAYS after the conference — or 20 days after any agreed extension for additional materials — the Regional Center sends a letter reversing, modifying or affirming.\n\nH. WHO DECIDES. The Regional Center Director or designee reviews the appeal, conducts the conference, and issues the written decision. The official must NOT have been directly involved in the decision being appealed. That decision is a FINAL DETERMINATION.\n\nI. AFTER THE APPEAL, the AE will:\n1. RENEW if the owner becomes compliant or prevails and the Regional Center or Satellite Office changes its decision;\n2. otherwise, begin obtaining REGULAR HCVs under PIH 2001-41(HA) or its successor;\n3. monitor voucher processing with the AE/CA, PIH and the owner; and\n4. TERMINATE the contract after vouchers are issued to eligible residents.\n\nTENANT NOTICE (13-4). The Regional Center or Satellite Office notifies ALL tenants receiving Section 8 assistance, in writing, of HUD's decision not to renew; Section 516(d) provides regular vouchers and reasonable moving expenses (Chapter 10 of this course).\n\nWorked example — the calendar. The owner receives the AE's refusal notice on March 3. It has until April 2 to object or cure. It objects on March 28. The AE affirms on April 15; the owner receives it April 17 and appeals April 24 (within 10 days), requesting a conference. The conference must be held by May 4 (10 calendar days after the request). The owner asks to submit an engineer's report; the parties agree on May 14. The Regional Center's decision is due by June 3 (20 days after the agreed May 14 extension). If the contract expires April 30, the AE provides a short-term renewal pending the appeal.\n\nTHE PRACTICAL ADVICE. The 30-day window is the owner's best chance. A CURE within 30 days ends the matter; an objection without a cure only starts a process that the owner, on these facts, often loses. If the problem is physical, spend the 30 days fixing it — and document the fix."},
  ]
},
{
  "id":"r15","unit":"ur5","number":15,
  "title":"Residual Receipts, RHS 515/8 & Old-Regulation HFA Projects",
  "subtitle":"Three special populations: residual receipts offsets, Rural Housing Service Section 515/8 renewals, and HFA projects whose 1975 HAP contracts ended at prepayment.",
  "sections":[
  {"heading":"Residual Receipts","body":"Chapter 10 of the Guide is short — it introduces residual receipts and points to the governing Notice — but the rule it states has real dollars attached.\n\nTHE RULE. Owners of Section 8 projects subject to the REVISED 1980 regulations at 24 CFR 880.205(e), 881.205(e) or 883.306(e) are reminded that at ANY TIME during a project's fiscal year, the AE may determine that project funds are more than needed for project operations, reserve requirements and permitted distributions. On that determination, the AE may require that ALL OR A PORTION of the funds in the project's RESIDUAL RECEIPTS ACCOUNT be used to:\n• REDUCE Housing Assistance Payments; or\n• for other project purposes.\n\nDETAILED GUIDANCE. Housing Notice H 2012-14, or subsequent Housing Notices, governs the treatment of residual receipts.\n\nWHAT A RESIDUAL RECEIPTS ACCOUNT IS. For projects with limited distributions, surplus cash beyond the permitted distribution does not go to the owner; it accumulates in a residual receipts account held for the project, released only with HUD approval. The Guide's rule lets the AE put that money to work — most directly by offsetting the Section 8 subsidy HUD would otherwise pay.\n\nWHERE RESIDUAL RECEIPTS SHOW UP ELSEWHERE IN THIS COURSE.\n• Distributions (Chapter 2): new regulation for-profit owners are limited to 6% (elderly) or 10% (non-elderly) of initial equity; nonprofits with new regulation contracts get no distributions without a waiver. Surplus cash above those limits is where residual receipts come from.\n• Budget-based rents (Chapter 3): amounts budgeted for debt service coverage may be drawn at year-end to pay allowable distributions or deferred developer fee from surplus cash, using the required Residual Receipts Note (HUD-91710M for nonprofit borrowers) or Promissory Note; REMAINING surplus cash goes to the residual receipts account (if the project has one).\n\nWorked example — an offset. Maple Terrace (revised 1980 regulations, nonprofit owner) has $412,000 in its residual receipts account. Its operating reserves are fully funded and it has no permitted distributions (nonprofit, new regulation). The AE determines that the funds exceed project needs and directs $250,000 to reduce HAP over the next year: if monthly HAP is $96,000, the offset covers roughly 2.6 months of subsidy, and HUD's outlay falls by $250,000.\n\nWorked example — other project purposes. The same project needs a $180,000 elevator modernization not covered by the reserve for replacement. The owner asks to use residual receipts. Under the Notice, the AE may approve use of residual receipts for this project purpose.\n\nCONSULTANT'S NOTE. Before a client with a large residual receipts balance plans a renewal or a refinancing, read the current residual receipts Notice. A balance that HUD may sweep to offset HAP is not equity the owner can count on — but a balance that can fund capital needs may reduce what the owner has to borrow."},
  {"heading":"RHS Section 515/8: Who They Are and Which Options They Have","body":"THE POPULATION (14-1). Rural Housing Service (RHS) Section 515 projects with Section 8 — \"515/8\" projects — represent a significant share of affordable housing in many rural communities. They are EXEMPT from debt restructuring under MAHRA (they are financed under Section 515 of the Housing Act of 1949 — Chapter 5 of this course, 6-1.A.2). They are eligible to renew under OPTION FOUR, but may request to renew under ANY option for which they are eligible at the time of renewal.\n\nTHE MUTM NOTE. If a 515/8 project is subject to a use agreement that the owner cannot eliminate by unilateral action, it is NOT eligible for Option One-A (3-3.B) — but the owner may still be able to renew under Option One-B (Chapter 4 of this course).\n\nRENT-SETTING AT RENEWAL. RHS projects are exempt from debt restructuring under Section 524(b). Like all other 524(b) projects, at initial AND subsequent renewal they are subject to the \"lesser of\" OCAF or budget-based test (14-2.E note; 6-2.A).\n\nPROCESSING (14-2).\nA. RHS-APPROVED BUDGETS. Owners requesting an Option Four renewal under 524(b)(1) submit their project budget APPROVED BY RHS. The AE/CA is NOT required to review and approve it — as long as RHS approved it, HUD/the CA accepts the budget-based rent.\n   Note: HUD does NOT accept the RHS utility analysis. The owner must submit a utility analysis meeting HUD requirements (Notice 2015-04 — Chapter 3 of this course).\nB. THE MARKET CAP AND THE RCS. RHS agreed in November 2010 that a budget-based adjustment requested at the Amend Rents calculation cannot result in rents above market as determined by the RCS. If the budget-based submission produces a \"0\" increase, or rents that do not exceed what OCAF would have produced, the owner need NOT submit an RCS to show rents are below market.\nC. DISTRIBUTIONS. The AE/CA accepts RHS-approved budgets reflecting the appropriate 8 PERCENT allowable owner's distribution on equity — or any higher level RHS approves as an incentive to prevent prepayment (RHS administrative Notice, April 12, 1999).\n   Note: if the owner is a for-profit NOT restricted by RHS at the State level, HUD cannot and will not limit owner distributions.\nD. NOTICE OF RENTS. The AE/CA notifies BOTH RHS and the owner of the new contract rents at renewal and at annual adjustments.\n\nWorked example 1 — the RCS question. A 515/8 project's current rent is $640; OCAF is 3.0% → $659.20. The RHS-approved budget supports $655. $655 ≤ $659.20 → no RCS needed. If the budget supported $690 (above the OCAF result), the owner would need an RCS showing $690 does not exceed market.\n\nWorked example 2 — \"lesser of\" at renewal. Same project at its renewal: OCAF result $659.20; RHS budget $655. Renewal rent = lesser = $655.\n\nWorked example 3 — the 8 percent. Owner equity recognized by RHS: $210,000. 8% × $210,000 = $16,800 annual distribution included in the RHS-approved budget; HUD accepts it. Compare a HUD new regulation for-profit non-elderly project: 10% of initial equity — a different program, a different rule."},
  {"heading":"RHS 515/8: Calendars, Short-Term Renewals and After the Loan","body":"ALIGNING THE CALENDARS (14-2.E). RHS projects run on a calendar-year accounting cycle; many HAP contracts do not. An owner may receive a SHORT-TERM RENEWAL to align the project's accounting cycle with the HAP anniversary: the AE/CA issues a short-term contract covering the months between the end of the current HAP contract and the end of the current accounting cycle — DECEMBER 31. When it expires, the owner is eligible for renewal under MAHRA as usual.\n\nRecall from Chapter 2 of this course: aligning the contract with the project's fiscal year end is one of the listed reasons for a short-term renewal (2-8.A.3), and short-term renewals run in whole months.\n\nTHE ANNUAL CALENDAR FOR A DECEMBER 31 CONTRACT (14-2.F). An owner with a HAP contract expiring December 31 is requested to:\n• submit its renewal request to HUD/the CA annually by SEPTEMBER 1 — it may submit a NON-approved budget at that time; and\n• submit the RHS-APPROVED budget to the local HUD Regional Center or Satellite Office/CA no later than NOVEMBER 15.\n\nWorked example — alignment. A 515/8 project's HAP contract expires April 30. Its RHS fiscal year ends December 31. The AE/CA issues an 8-month short-term contract, May 1 – December 31. The next renewal runs from January 1. Going forward the owner submits by September 1 and delivers the RHS-approved budget by November 15 each year.\n\nTHE SHORT-TERM RENT. Because this is a short-term renewal of a Section 524 contract, if the project is OCAF-eligible the rent is a PRO-RATED OCAF (2-9.B): line R ÷ 12 × 8. With an OCAF of 3.6%: 3.6 ÷ 12 = 0.30 × 8 = 2.40%. (No budget-based adjustment is permitted for a short-term renewal.)\n\nAFTER THE RHS LOAN IS PAID IN FULL (14-2.G).\n1. A budget-based rent adjustment request goes DIRECTLY to the AE/CA, completed under Chapter 2 of the Guide (2-15) — no more RHS approval shortcut.\n2. At expiration, the owner submits a renewal request directly to HUD/the CA, and may elect ANY renewal option available at that time for which the project is eligible.\n\nThat second point matters: with the Section 515 loan gone, the project is no longer exempt on that ground. Re-run the classification drill from Chapter 1 of this course — is there an FHA-insured or HUD-held mortgage now? Are rents above or below market? The answers may put the project into Option Two, Option Four on a different basis (6-1.B.1, no federal mortgage), or MUTM.\n\nCHECKLIST FOR A 515/8 CLIENT\n☐ RHS-approved budget in hand (no HUD review needed — but RHS's approval is required).\n☐ HUD-standard utility analysis (RHS's will not be accepted).\n☐ Does the budget exceed the OCAF result? If so, an RCS.\n☐ Distribution at 8% of equity (or higher as RHS approved)?\n☐ Calendar aligned to December 31? Submission by September 1; RHS budget by November 15.\n☐ Use agreement non-eliminable? Then One-A is out; consider One-B.\n☐ Loan paid off? Then the RHS shortcuts end and the options re-open."},
  {"heading":"Old-Regulation HFA Projects: The 1975 HAP Form Problem","body":"WHICH PROJECTS (16-1). Section 8 projects under the \"old regulation\" State Housing Finance Agency (HFA) form of HAP contract for New Construction or Substantial Rehabilitation — the NOVEMBER 1975 version of HUD-52645A (the \"1975 HAP Form\") — where the original, permanent financing provided by an HFA (the \"Financing\") was, or will be, PREPAID IN FULL.\nA small number of HFA contracts lack the HUD-52645A form number but contain its key provisions retyped in a different format; those are ALSO covered.\n\nTHE PROBLEM. HUD has determined that the 1975 HAP Form contains language causing it to TERMINATE AUTOMATICALLY upon full prepayment of the original, permanent Financing. So any project under the 1975 form whose Financing has been fully prepaid has been operating since the prepayment date with NO WRITTEN HAP CONTRACT in place.\n\nTHE IMPLIED CONTRACT. HUD has further determined that where the owner and the HFA continued, after prepayment, to perform their contractual roles as if the written contract were still in place, their CONDUCT gives rise to an IMPLIED CONTRACT — with terms identical to the written but now-expired HAP contract. That is why these owners kept getting paid.\n\nWHY HUD ISSUED CHAPTER 16. To create an orderly process for continuing housing assistance payments to owners who choose to stay in Section 8. Section 16-2 gives them three options.\n\nHOW TO SPOT ONE.\n• The HAP contract is on HUD-52645A, November 1975 version — or looks like it with the key provisions retyped.\n• It is an HFA-financed New Construction or Substantial Rehabilitation project.\n• The original, permanent HFA financing was paid off (often in a bond refunding or a refinancing), or will be.\n\nWorked example. Brookside Manor was built in 1979 with HFA bond financing and a 1975-form HAP contract running to 2019 — the bonds' original maturity. The owner refinanced and paid off the HFA bonds in 2003. Under HUD's determination, the written HAP contract terminated in 2003. The HFA kept administering it and the owner kept performing, so an implied contract has existed since 2003 on the same terms. Brookside's file has no written contract after 2003 — and the owner may not know that.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS TO A CONSULTANT. An owner (or a buyer doing due diligence) who assumes a written HAP contract is in force may be wrong. Lenders, buyers and auditors want a written contract. Chapter 16 is how to get one."},
  {"heading":"Old-Regulation HFA Projects: The Three Options","body":"Where the Financing has been or will be prepaid, the owner may choose one of three options (16-2).\n\nA. AMEND THE HAP CONTRACT. Extend the term to the ORIGINALLY SCHEDULED MATURITY DATE of the Financing, using Form HUD-9647, \"Extension Amendment to Old Regulation State Agency Housing Assistance Payments Contract\" (\"Extension Amendment\"), VERBATIM. No variations in the contract language are permitted without the PRIOR WRITTEN APPROVAL of the HUD Multifamily Regional Center Director with jurisdiction. Execution of the Extension Amendment by BOTH the owner and the HFA will:\n(1) be deemed to REINSTATE the 1975 HAP Form of contract; and\n(2) amend it by EXTENDING the term through the originally scheduled maturity date of the Financing that was prepaid.\n\nB. RENEW UNDER MAHRA. Regardless of when the Financing was prepaid, the owner may renew under MAHRA under ANY option for which the project is eligible.\n• The Preservation Exhibit and HUD-93184 are NOT required.\n• The owner completes HUD-9624 (indicating the option) and the corresponding Renewal Worksheet.\n• If the option requires an RCS — Options One, Two or Three — the RCS must be dated NO MORE THAN 90 DAYS before the owner submits HUD-9624.\n• Eligibility is determined as of the \"DATE OF SUBMISSION\" on page 1 of HUD-9624. (Compare the general rule in 2-2.B: eligibility at contract expiration or mutual termination. Here there is no clean expiration date, so the Guide uses the submission date.)\n• The MAHRA contract term begins on the FIRST DAY OF THE MONTH FOLLOWING the month in which the parties execute it. If the owner and HFA sign in different months, the term begins the first day of the month after the LAST party signs. For PROSPECTIVE prepayments, the term begins on the PREPAYMENT DATE.\n\nC. OPT OUT. Select Option Six on HUD-9624. If more time is needed to satisfy the one-year notice, the HFA and the owner may enter a short-term contract, not exceeding one year, at CURRENT contract rents, using the Basic Renewal Contract (HUD-9636).\n\nTIMELINESS (16-3). Owners are encouraged to submit a completed Extension Amendment or HUD-9624, with supporting documents, to the HFA within 120 DAYS of the contract anniversary date.\n\nWorked example 1 — Option A. Brookside's bonds originally matured in 2019, now past. An Extension Amendment would extend to the original maturity — a date already gone — so Option A buys nothing for Brookside. Option A is most useful where the original maturity is still in the future.\n\nWorked example 2 — Option B dates. Brookside chooses Option Two. Its RCS is dated February 10; HUD-9624 is submitted March 25 (43 days — within 90). Eligibility is tested as of March 25. The owner signs the renewal contract on June 28; the HFA signs July 6. The term begins August 1 — the first day of the month after the LAST signature.\n\nWorked example 3 — a prospective prepayment. Oakmont's HFA bonds will be prepaid on October 15. It renews under Option Two in advance; the MAHRA contract term begins on October 15, the prepayment date — so there is no gap.\n\nCHOOSING. Option A preserves the old contract's terms (often favorable 1970s-era rent mechanics, AAF adjustments) until the original maturity — no RCS, no MAHRA option analysis. Option B brings the project into the modern framework — MUTM if rents are below market, a 20-year contract, increased distributions — at the cost of an RCS and the MAHRA rules. Option C ends the subsidy subject to the one-year notice and enhanced vouchers. Model all three."},
  ]
},
{
  "id":"r16","unit":"ur5","number":16,
  "title":"Preservation: Capital Repairs & Transfer Programs",
  "subtitle":"Nonprofit definitions, the 20-year use agreement, rent increases for repairs and acquisitions, environmental review, and purchaser requirements.",
  "sections":[
  {"heading":"What Chapter 15 Offers — and to Whom","body":"PURPOSE (15-1). One of HUD's primary goals is the long-term preservation of affordable housing. Chapter 15 encourages REHABILITATION — the \"Capital Repairs\" program — and ACQUISITION — the \"Transfer\" program — through budget-based or MUTM rent increases at renewal.\n\nWHO USES WHICH OPTION.\n• Nonprofit owners or purchasers → Option Two (renewals for projects with rents at or below market).\n• Nonprofit owners or purchasers meeting one of the three Section 3-6 criteria → Option One (MUTM).\n• For-profit owners → Option One or Option Two.\n• For-profit entities proposing to ACQUIRE AND REHABILITATE → must renew under Option One (MUTM).\nThe chapter has four parts: the nonprofit owner definition, general criteria for both programs, the Capital Repairs Program, and the Transfer Program.\n\nNONPROFIT OWNER/PURCHASER (15-2.A). The AE must determine that the nonprofit:\n1. is financially solvent, with no open or unresolved audit findings or findings from analyses of its audited annual financial statements;\n2. has a tax exemption ruling under Section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986:\n   a. a nonprofit APPLYING for the ruling is eligible, but increased rents are WITHHELD until it provides evidence the ruling has been issued;\n   b. exceptions: (1) a nonprofit not previously required to have a 501(c)(3) ruling to participate in HUD programs; (2) Limited-Equity Cooperatives not eligible for 501(c)(3);\n3. has a Board of Directors resolution authorizing the additional debt to purchase and/or rehabilitate the project.\n\nUNACCEPTABLE NONPROFITS (15-2.B):\n1. a public body or an instrumentality of a public body; or\n2. an entity whose organizational documents permit any part of its net earnings to inure to the benefit of any private shareholder, contributor or individual.\n\nNONPROFIT-CONTROLLED FOR-PROFIT ENTITIES (15-3). A nonprofit may form a for-profit entity for a project — for example, to obtain and syndicate Low Income Housing Tax Credits. For this Guide, \"Nonprofit Owner\" includes:\n1. a limited partnership with one or more nonprofit general partners, or a sole general partner wholly owned and controlled by one or more nonprofits; or\n2. a limited liability company with one or more nonprofit managers or managing members, or a sole manager or managing member wholly owned or controlled by one or more nonprofits.\nThe nonprofit must meet 15-2.A. Its renewal options are in 15-5.E.3: if eligible, it may renew under Option One (MUTM), and the Option One rent-setting mechanism then overrides 15-6.\n\nWorked examples — acceptable nonprofit?\n• A 501(c)(3) community development corporation, solvent, clean audits, board resolution authorizing a $6 million acquisition loan → acceptable.\n• A county housing authority → a public body → unacceptable as a nonprofit owner under Chapter 15.\n• A nonprofit whose bylaws allow surplus to be paid to its founding members → net earnings inure to individuals → unacceptable.\n• A newly formed nonprofit with its 501(c)(3) application pending → eligible, but its rent increase is withheld until the IRS ruling is in hand.\n• A tax credit LP whose sole GP is an LLC wholly owned by a qualifying 501(c)(3) → a \"Nonprofit Owner\" for the Guide."},
  {"heading":"General Criteria: Eligible Contracts, the Use Agreement and the 20-Year Contract","body":"WHICH CONTRACTS (15-4.A). Most multifamily projects with either:\n1. an ORIGINAL Section 8 HAP contract (not yet renewed under MAHRA) with rents at or below comparable market; or\n2. a MAHRA Renewal Contract issued under Section 524(a) or (b) with rents at or below comparable market.\n\nRENT TESTS BY OPTION (15-4.B).\n1. Option Two: current rents must be AT OR BELOW market at renewal.\n2. Option One: current rents must be BELOW market at renewal.\n\nWHO CANNOT APPLY (15-4.C):\n1. Section 8 Mod Rehab projects administered by PIH;\n2. Section 8 Mod Rehab SRO projects administered by CPD;\n3. contracts that closed under Mark-to-Market and were renewed with a Full M2M Renewal Contract under Section 515 of MAHRA;\n4. an original (non-MAHRA) contract eligible to renew under MAHRA but with rents ABOVE comparable market;\n5. a 524(a) or 524(b) renewal contract with rents ABOVE comparable market.\n\nREDUCING RENTS TO GET IN (15-5.C.3). Owners of above-market projects whose HAP contract allows (i) a discretionary comparability adjustment within each five-year term, or (ii) a fifth-year comparability adjustment, may ask to have rents reduced to market to participate and renew under Option Two:\na. at the discretionary or fifth-year adjustment, the AE/CA reduces rents to market;\nb. the contract is then terminated and renewed for 20 years under Option Two (15-6.B);\nc. the AE attaches HUD-93181 or HUD-93182 (formerly Appendices 15-2A and 15-2B), establishing the \"as is\" and \"after rehab\" rents.\nNote: HUD lacks authority to let a project reduce rents to BELOW market so that it can renew under MUTM.\n\nTHE USE AGREEMENT (15-5.A). At renewal, the owner must accept a 20-YEAR RECORDED USE AGREEMENT requiring the current and future owners to accept any Section 8 contract offered by HUD for the next 20 years (Form HUD-90055). If the project already has a Use Agreement, its term is EXTENDED by an additional 20 years — the Guide's example: a Use Agreement ending in 2015 extends to 2035 — and the existing agreement may need to be modified to include the new conditions.\n\nTHE 20-YEAR CONTRACT (15-5.B). The owner or purchaser must accept a 20-year Section 8 contract, subject to annual appropriations.\n\nEARLY TERMINATION (15-5.C).\n1. HUD and the owner may terminate by mutual agreement most original HAP contracts and Section 524 contracts with rents at or below market, as long as the new contract is 20 years, the owner agrees to renew at the end of the 20 years for a term equal to the remaining term of the terminated contract, and — for non-MAHRA contracts — signs HUD-93184.\n2. NOT eligible for early termination to use these programs: (a) Section 514 or 515 MAHRA contracts; (b) Section 524(e)(1) Demo contracts; (c) Section 524(e)(2) Preservation contracts; (d) Rental Assistance Demonstration (RAD) contracts.\n\nCOMBINING (15-5.D). If possible, owners MUST combine multiple contracts or stages (2-10).\n\nRENEWAL OPTIONS (15-5.E).\n1. The owner or purchaser renews under Option Two.\n2. A for-profit entity in the Transfer program, or in both Transfer and Capital Repairs, must renew under Option One, assuming MUTM eligibility.\n3. A nonprofit-controlled for-profit entity, if eligible, may renew under Option One — and Option One's rent-setting overrides 15-6.\n\nWorked example — reducing to market. Hillcrest's rents are 7% above market; its original HAP contract has a fifth-year comparability adjustment due next March. A nonprofit buyer wants to acquire and rehabilitate it under Chapter 15. In March the AE/CA reduces rents to market; the contract is terminated and renewed for 20 years under Option Two; HUD-93181 or 93182 is attached with as-is and after-rehab rents. Hillcrest could NOT instead cut rents slightly below market to qualify for MUTM — HUD lacks that authority."},
  {"heading":"Rent Increases: Submissions, Review and Effective Dates","body":"WHAT THE OWNER SUBMITS — OPTION ONE (15-6.A).\n1. An RCS with \"AS IS\" market rents and \"AFTER REHAB\" market rents, assuming all repairs/rehabilitation were complete as of the RCS date.\n2. A detailed description of the transaction, including:\n   a. TRANSFER: (1) a letter of intent to sell the project to an eligible nonprofit or eligible for-profit; and (2) the cost of recapitalizing the reserve for replacement account;\n   b. CAPITAL REPAIRS: the cost of (1) modest repairs and rehabilitation (lead-based paint, energy-efficient equipment, repairs) and recapitalizing the reserve for replacement account, OR (2) SUBSTANTIAL REHABILITATION — defined as hard costs exceeding $6,500 per unit (or a later HUD threshold) TIMES the High Cost Percentage for the area.\n3. A Project Capital Needs Assessment (PCNA) or Comprehensive Needs Assessment (CNA) (15-9).\n4. A detailed sources-and-uses statement.\n\nOPTION TWO (15-6.B). Everything in 15-6.A, PLUS a request for a budget-based rent increase NOT TO EXCEED comparable market rents, to pay for the transaction's costs — including new debt and debt service coverage (see the note after 2-15.B).\n\nREVIEW (15-6.C–D). For either option, the AE does the INITIAL screening of the RCS; the CA is responsible for the SUBSTANTIVE review. The AE notifies the owner or purchaser of the MAXIMUM new rent levels when processing is complete.\n\nWorked example — substantial rehab. Area High Cost Percentage: 240%. Threshold: $6,500 × 2.40 = $15,600 hard costs per unit. A 72-unit project with $1,296,000 in hard costs = $18,000 per unit → substantial rehabilitation. At $1,008,000 ($14,000/unit) it would be modest repairs.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE OF NEW RENTS (15-6.E).\n1. CAPITAL REPAIRS with a loan:\n   a. a loan NOT requiring full debt service at closing → the final \"after rehab\" rents take effect only when the PCNA is complete, financing is approved, and (if applicable) critical repairs are done. The owner signs HUD-93181, Addendum to Renewal Contract under Option One or Option Two for Capital Repairs and/or Acquisition Costs.\n   b. a loan REQUIRING full debt service at closing — such as Fannie Mae or Freddie Mac Mod Rehab programs, or FHA 223(f) — the Regional Center/Satellite Office can allow post-rehab rents to take effect AT CLOSING. The owner signs HUD-93182, the Addendum for Capital Repairs and/or Acquisition — Post-Rehabilitation Rents at Closing.\n2. TRANSFER → the final new rents take effect once the transfer is approved and completed.\n3. BLENDED (transfer + capital repairs) → rents adjust after the STRICTEST requirement in 1 or 2 has been met.\n\nRecall from Chapter 12 of this course: included services planned in a Capital Repairs transaction can support the after-rehab rents — and if not implemented, rents may be reduced under the HUD-93181/93182 addendum.\n\nUNASSISTED UNITS (15-7). To protect low- and moderate-income tenants in OTHER HUD-subsidized units in the project — Rent Supplement, RAP, BMIR, Section 236 (Handbook 4350.3 REV-1) — the TENANT'S PORTION of the rent may be increased by NO MORE THAN 10 PERCENT because of the transaction. Owners raising those rents must comply with 24 CFR Part 245 notice requirements.\n\nCAP ON MARKET RENTS (15-8). The AE will not lower the comparable market rents in the RCS to reflect any use restriction — tax credit rents, for example. (The same rule as 2-5.C.)\n\nWorked example — 15-7. A BMIR tenant in a Section 236 project pays $720. The transaction's budget would raise that tenant's rent to $820. Cap: $720 × 1.10 = $792. The tenant's portion may rise no more than $72 because of the transaction, with Part 245 notice."},
  {"heading":"PCNAs, Reserves, Owner Acceptability and Environmental Review","body":"PCNA AND RESERVES (15-9).\nA. For both HUD-insured and conventionally financed projects, the owner/purchaser's MORTGAGEE must submit a PCNA prepared under Housing Notice 2012-27 or later guidance. Projects legally required to submit a CNA may submit a current CNA instead — updated or resubmitted within the previous 12 MONTHS.\n   Note: for a non-insured project with an old regulation, LMSA, Property Disposition or Pension Fund Section 8 contract, or a partially assisted project, NO reserve for replacement account is required unless the existing or proposed financing requires one for the life of the mortgage. Projects with NEW regulation contracts MUST maintain the RFR.\nB. If the transaction includes no new debt, the owner/purchaser assumes the mortgagee's role.\nC. For non-insured projects, the AE may review the PCNA for adequacy and recommend revisions.\n(15-10 is reserved.)\n\nTHE CAPITAL REPAIRS PROGRAM COVERS (15-11): (A) modest repairs and rehabilitation (lead-based paint, energy-efficient developments, repairs) and recapitalizing the replacement reserve escrow; or (B) substantial rehabilitation.\n\nOWNER ACCEPTABILITY (15-12.A). The owner must be:\n1. in good standing and not subject to administrative sanctions — debarment, suspension, unresolved adverse audit findings, or a letter of findings under a fair housing or civil rights authority where it failed to correct material violations of HUD rules;\n2. in compliance with the current Regulatory Agreement, Note and Mortgage; and\n3. current in debt service and all payments, including the reserve for replacement (or current under a workout agreement).\nNote: a current owner subject to administrative sanctions may still participate as the SELLER in the Transfer program — but may NOT receive funds from the transaction until all costs of bringing the project to an acceptable standard are covered.\n\nENVIRONMENTAL AND ACCESSIBILITY DOCUMENTATION (15-12.B–C). The owner submits documentation with its Capital Repairs application for the AE's review under 24 CFR Part 50. ALL repair or rehabilitation actions under Capital Repairs require environmental review, and HUD policy LIMITS activities by any direct or indirect party until the review is complete. Owners also submit documentation for accessibility review under Section 504 and the Fair Housing Act (with the Regional FHEO Office or Program Center).\n\nPROJECT ELIGIBILITY — ENVIRONMENTAL (15-13.A).\n1. The environmental review must be COMPLETE BEFORE the AE approves capital repair plans.\n2. The AE documents compliance in HEROS (HUD Environmental Review Online System) — Form HUD 4128 (guidance: MAP Guide Chapter 9; Handbook 1390.2).\n3. Field/Regional Environmental Clearance Officers can assist.\n4. CATEGORICAL EXCLUSION from NEPA under 24 CFR 50.20(a)(2) — still subject to the other authorities in 24 CFR 50.4 and a categorically excluded review in HEROS — for rehabilitation where:\n   1) unit density does not change by more than 20 PERCENT;\n   2) there is no change in land use from residential to non-residential; and\n   3) the estimated cost of rehabilitation is LESS THAN 75 PERCENT of the total estimated cost of replacement after rehabilitation.\n5. Anything not meeting item 4 requires a full NEPA environmental review under 50.4.\n6. A NEW PHASE I ESA (ASTM E 1527-05 or most recent) plus a VAPOR ENCROACHMENT SCREEN (ASTM E 2600-10 or most recent) are required if the activity involves: (a) significant ground disturbance or construction not contemplated in the original application; (b) a land-use change not contemplated by the original risk-based mitigation; (c) site expansion or addition; or (d) other activities that may create contaminant exposure pathways.\n7. LEAD-BASED PAINT: HUD's testing and abatement requirements apply to housing built BEFORE 1978 (24 CFR Part 35).\n8. ASBESTOS (HUD-insured): EPA and OSHA requirements when renovation or demolition disturbs asbestos or damaged friable asbestos is present; use EPA-trained, accredited contractors; 40 CFR Subpart M (especially 61.145) and 29 CFR 1926.1101; state and local rules also apply.\n9. PCBs (HUD-insured): the Toxic Substances Control Act (15 U.S.C. 2605(e)) and 40 CFR Part 761 — transformers must be inspected and replaced if PCBs are found; a utility employee can inspect at the owner's request.\n10. Where the Capital Repairs application accompanies an FHA mortgage insurance application, the MAP Guide Chapter 9 environmental review satisfies this section.\n11. Chapter 15 rehabilitation does NOT trigger Davis-Bacon prevailing wages (Davis-Bacon applies under certain HUD mortgage insurance programs).\nB. A RELOCATION PLAN acceptable to HUD, including continuing reasonable accommodations for persons with disabilities.\nC. Physical ACCESSIBILITY compliance — 24 CFR 8.23 and, for covered multifamily dwellings, 24 CFR Part 100 — reviewed with FHEO.\n\nWorked example — categorical exclusion. Rehab of 96 units into 104 (density +8.3%), all residential, rehab cost $9.1 million against a post-rehab replacement cost of $14.2 million (64%) → categorically excluded from NEPA; complete the CatEx review in HEROS. If the cost were $11.0 million (77.5%) → full NEPA review."},
  {"heading":"The Transfer Program","body":"WHAT IT DOES (15-14). HUD permits a rent adjustment to facilitate a change in ownership:\n• Under OPTION TWO — a budget-based rent request, with new rents not exceeding comparable market — for a transfer from a for-profit or limited-dividend owner to a NONPROFIT or nonprofit-controlled entity, or from a nonprofit to ANOTHER nonprofit or nonprofit-controlled entity.\n• Under OPTION ONE — with new rents not exceeding comparable market — for a transfer from a for-profit or limited-dividend owner to a FOR-PROFIT or limited-dividend owner, or from a nonprofit to a for-profit or limited-dividend owner.\n\nIn short: the BUYER's type drives the option. A nonprofit (or nonprofit-controlled) buyer uses Option Two; a for-profit buyer uses Option One (and must be MUTM-eligible — 15-5.E.2).\n\nPURCHASER REQUIREMENTS (15-15).\nA. MANAGERIAL EXPERIENCE owning and/or operating multifamily projects, or significant activities providing decent housing affordable to very low-, low- and/or moderate-income families.\nB. NONDISCRIMINATION compliance under 24 CFR 5.105(a). Purchasers disclose lawsuits, charges, cause determinations or letters of findings related to discrimination; a purchaser that has not resolved them to HUD's satisfaction is INELIGIBLE.\nC. GOOD STANDING — not subject to administrative sanctions (debarment, suspension, unresolved adverse audit findings, or failure to correct material violations of HUD rules).\n\nPROJECT ELIGIBILITY WITHOUT REPAIRS (15-16). A Transfer WITHOUT repair or rehabilitation requires a physical inspection under the Uniform Physical Condition Standards (24 CFR Part 5) by REAC with a score GREATER THAN 60 and no uncorrected Exigent Health and Safety violations. The project must NOT be designated a \"troubled project.\"\n\nTRANSFER OF PHYSICAL ASSETS (15-17). For a HUD-insured transaction, HUD WAIVES THE TRANSFER FEE to encourage nonprofit transfers; follow the TPA instructions in Handbook 4350.1.\n\nTHE SELLER WITH SANCTIONS (15-12.A note). Recall: a current owner subject to administrative sanctions may still SELL through the Transfer program, but receives no proceeds until the costs of bringing the project to an acceptable standard are covered. The Transfer program is, among other things, a way to move a property away from a problem owner.\n\nPUTTING A TRANSACTION TOGETHER — CASE. Laurel Gardens: 84 units, original Section 8 HAP contract, rents 6% below market, REAC 64 with no open EH&S items, not troubled. The for-profit seller is under an LDP. A 501(c)(3) regional nonprofit with a 20-year track record wants to buy it with an FHA 223(f) loan and do $1.1 million of modest repairs.\n• Program: blended Transfer + Capital Repairs. Buyer type → Option Two.\n• Nonprofit acceptability (15-2): solvent, 501(c)(3), board resolution → yes.\n• Purchaser requirements (15-15): experience, no unresolved discrimination matters, good standing → yes.\n• Contract: terminate by mutual agreement and renew for 20 years (15-5.C.1), with a 20-year recorded Use Agreement (HUD-90055).\n• Submissions: RCS with as-is and after-rehab rents; letter of intent; RFR recapitalization cost; repair costs; PCNA (from the FHA lender); sources and uses; budget-based increase not exceeding market.\n• Environmental: HEROS review complete before the AE approves the repair plan; built 1972 → lead-based paint rules apply.\n• Rents effective: blended → after the strictest requirement. With a 223(f) loan requiring full debt service at closing, the Regional Center may allow post-rehab rents at closing under HUD-93182 — but the transfer must also be approved and completed.\n• The seller's LDP: it may sell, but receives no proceeds until the costs of bringing the project to an acceptable standard are covered.\n• Unassisted HUD-subsidized units: any tenant-portion increase from the transaction capped at 10%, with Part 245 notice.\n• Transfer fee: waived (nonprofit transfer, HUD-insured)."},
  ]
},
];

